window.TEAMS = [{"team":"UOregon","id":"6041","section":"Overgrad","country":"United States","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/","projectTitle":"AquaFlock","tagline":"E. coli engineered to sequester groundwater nitrate as cyanophycin, then flocculate so the nitrogen can be harvested back as fertilizer","summary":"UOregon engineered E. coli to pull excess environmental nitrate into the nitrogen storage polymer cyanophycin and, once a cyanophycin threshold is reached, to express flocculation proteins so cells sink and can be harvested as a fertilizer substitute, closing the nitrogen loop. The target users are rural well owners and farmers in Oregon whose water is rarely tested. The education practice is the standout: three original curricula built for three audiences with increasing prior knowledge, developed through an explicit Design-Build-Test-Learn loop and grounded in Universal Design for Learning plus STEAM. The team also built an offline desktop search engine indexing the full text of 1,354 iGEM team wikis and 20,761 pages from 2022-2025.","awards":["Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Education itself was run as a DBTL engineering cycle, with a documented Reflective Theory of Change diagram and a standardised success rubric (engagement, science understanding, creativity), both adapted from an Oregon State University STEM Research Center presentation at the Polar STEAM Summit 2026. Learning objectives were written against the measurable verbs of Bloom's Taxonomy (Remember, Understand, Apply, Analyze, Evaluate, Create).","dataviz":"Hand-illustrated scientific diagrams throughout: all educational materials were digitally illustrated by hand by the team, including a central-dogma comic, illustrated workbooks, and illustrated synthetic-biology-tool cards used as a tactile matching activity.","datascience":"Post-workshop feedback surveys with section-level ranking; for Workshop 3, 8 of 15 respondents had heard of synthetic biology before attending, and bioethics and science communication ranked as the favourite sections, with the illustrated workbook, real-world examples and small-group discussions named the most effective learning tools.","software":"iGEM Wiki Search: a desktop application that searches the full text of 1,354 team wikis and 20,761 pages from the 2022-2025 competitions from a single search box, running offline with no server. It automates wiki collection and processing, builds a full-text index, ranks whole projects rather than individual pages, crawls responsibly, handles JavaScript-only wikis, and was tested cross-platform. The team benchmarked it explicitly against iGEM's Phoenix Project (metadata only) and iGEM Munich 2024's Data Explorer (2019 wikis only, no source links), and links every result back to its original page.","stack":["desktop application (offline)","full-text indexing","web crawler with JavaScript rendering","Universal Design for Learning (UDL) framework","STEAM curriculum design","Bloom's Taxonomy"],"techniques":["cyanophycin nitrogen-storage pathway engineering","flocculation-protein inducible circuit","split biosensor","nitrate sequestration assays","AquaFlock hardware settling system"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"UOregon's central design principle is quotable and transferable: 'Accessibility should lower the barrier of entry into complex science, not the complexity of the science itself.' Instead of one generic outreach talk, the team wrote three original curricula for three audiences with increasing prior knowledge and ran them as three DBTL iterations across June, July and October 2026, carrying the lessons of each into the next. Workshop 1 ('Intro to Synthetic Biology') was a one-hour session plus an illustrated take-home worksheet delivered to 30 high school students at the Oregon Humane Society summer camp; students reviewed the central dogma and plasmid parts and then designed their own engineered bacterium to solve a problem of their choosing. Post-event debriefs with students and camp staff produced four concrete fixes (establish prior knowledge first, add more real-world relevance, shorten transitions to reduce cognitive load, create more peer-interaction points), which were fed directly into Workshop 2. Workshop 2 ('Engineering SynBio Solutions') was a two-hour session for 20 high school students at SAIL (Student Academy to Inspire Learning), an existing University of Oregon STEM summer camp for students from diverse learning backgrounds; materials were a fully illustrated workbook, a hand-drawn comic that told the central dogma as a story, slides, and hands-on activities, with each of five sections framed by its own guiding question and sequenced to scaffold from foundational biology to CRISPR and plasmids to genetic circuits to experimental design. Workshop 3 ('SynBio Solutions to Real-World Problems') was a two-hour session for 20 first- and second-year undergraduate science students at the University of Oregon, funded by a University of Oregon Knight Campus Graduate Student Association outreach mini-grant; because this audience already had foundational biology, the team swapped the comic for a matching-game refresher, added directed evolution and de novo protein design alongside CRISPR and plasmids, and then extended into two sections the earlier workshops could not reach: small-group bioethics analysis of three real-world synthetic biology applications, and a science-communication activity in which students decorated tote bags to explain what they had learned to an audience of their own choosing. Evidence of impact is tied to the standardised rubric rather than headcount alone: engagement, understanding and creativity were tracked across iterations through direct post-workshop feedback forms and indirect observation, Workshop 2 students asked unprompted questions beyond the taught material (one asked whether CRISPR and plasmids could be used at once), and the Workshop 3 survey ranked bioethics and science communication highest. Beyond workshops, the team published a comic illustrating the AquaFlock project's journey and released the iGEM Wiki Search desktop tool specifically to make it easier for future teams to find and build on predecessors' work - an education contribution aimed at the iGEM community itself.","ihp":"Human Practices was framed around rural equity: people living outside major cities receive less financial backing, less testing of wells and waterways, and less proactive healthcare, so the project is designed as a circular chain of agricultural, health and economic support for communities marginalised by municipal systems. The team mapped a four-step implementation path (in the home, then at the agricultural source, then harvest, then reduce/reuse/recycle) and stated the striking design goal that 'our product is made to eventually become obsolete' once nitrate levels fall out of the unsafe range. A field trip to the Metropolitan Wastewater Management Commission regional wastewater treatment facility serving Eugene and Springfield let the team interview plant operators, who confirmed nitrate control (alongside PFAS and silicon) is an unsolved challenge, validating a gap their smaller-scale system could fill. Four UN SDGs were adopted as focal areas: 3 (good health), 4 (quality education), 6 (clean water and sanitation) and 12 (responsible consumption and production).","realWorld":"The intended users are rural Oregon well owners and farmers; harvested flocs become a local fertilizer substitute, localising fertilizer access and cutting nitrate at source. Context established on the description page: roughly half of applied nitrogen fertilizer is taken up by plants and the rest leaches as nitrate, driving eutrophication, harmful algal blooms and hypoxia as well as direct human health risk from contaminated drinking water.","partners":["Oregon Humane Society (summer camp, Workshop 1 venue and audience)","SAIL - Student Academy to Inspire Learning, University of Oregon STEM summer camp (Workshop 2)","University of Oregon Knight Campus Graduate Student Association (outreach mini-grant funding Workshop 3)","Metropolitan Wastewater Management Commission (MWMC) Regional Wastewater Treatment Facility, Eugene-Springfield","Oregon State University STEM Research Center (Reflective Theory of Change and success-measurement frameworks)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp1-main-photo.avif","caption":"Workshop 1: one-hour intro to synthetic biology for 30 high school students at the Oregon Humane Society camp","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp2-main-photo.avif","caption":"Workshop 2 at the SAIL STEM summer camp: 20 high school students working through the illustrated workbook and comic","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp1-wkst.avif","caption":"The hand-illustrated take-home worksheet from Workshop 1, covering the central dogma and plasmid parts","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/wksp3-1.avif","caption":"Workshop 3 for 20 undergraduate science students, adding bioethics discussion and a tote-bag science-communication activity","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/measuring-success.avif","caption":"The team's standardised measure of success across workshop iterations: engagement, science understanding and creativity","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Run outreach as a DBTL cycle with a fixed success rubric: build three differentiated versions of the same curriculum for audiences at different prior-knowledge levels, debrief with the host staff after each delivery, and score every iteration on the same three criteria (engagement, understanding, creativity) so improvement is demonstrable rather than asserted.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/field-trip","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/sustainability"],"rank":7},{"team":"UOttawa","id":"6077","section":"Undergrad","country":"Canada","village":"Bioremediation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/","projectTitle":"Nulfur","tagline":"Break bonds, not the fuel: engineered biodesulfurization as a polishing step for refractory sulfur in diesel.","summary":"Nulfur rebuilds the four-enzyme 4S biodesulfurization pathway (DszA, DszB, DszC plus a flavin reductase) from Rhodococcus erythropolis inside Pseudomonas putida KT2440, targeting dibenzothiophene (DBT) — the refractory sulfur compound that conventional hydrodesulfurization struggles to remove without running at 300-400 C and 3-6 MPa. Rather than replacing hydrodesulfurization, the team reframed the project after industry consultation as a modular side-stream polishing step on diesel. The project won on the strength of its alternative chassis work: because P. putida is a non-model organism, the team built the missing toolkit from scratch (a Golden Gate-compatible broad-host-range vector, a measured four-promoter set, and a working heat-shock transformation protocol) and published it as a handbook for other teams. That platform contribution, paired with an unusually deep dry lab (COBRApy genome-scale modelling, a FoldX/ThermoMPNN protein engineering pipeline, and an agentic AI research platform called Cofactor), won Best Alternative Platform and Best Wiki and Best Bioremediation Project nominations.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Alternative Platform — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Bioremediation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three distinct computational layers. (1) Genome-scale metabolic modelling: the published P. putida KT2440 model iJN1463 was extended in COBRApy with the heterologous 4S pathway reactions (DszA/B/C/D), and flux balance analysis solved through GLPK over a 1000-point sweep of the DBT exchange flux bound showed metabolism never bottlenecks uptake, while forcing DszB flux revealed an NADH-limited ceiling of 9.80 mmol gDCW-1 h-1 that formate co-feeding raises 25.5% to 12.30. (2) A first-principles mass-transfer model of the biphasic reactor combining Hinze-Kolmogorov droplet-breakup scaling, film theory at the stagnant-sphere limit (Sh = 2), micellar solubilization above the CMC and Michaelis-Menten uptake, solved by bisection for the steady-state free DBT concentration; it predicts rhamnolipid raises effective DBT availability 2.8- to 34-fold. (3) A protein engineering pipeline that screened over 1,100 candidate substitutions in DszB and DszC using ConSurf conservation grades, MD-derived backbone RMSF, and an ensemble of FoldX (empirical force field) plus ThermoMPNN (deep learning on ProteinMPNN embeddings) for ddG, then validated catalytic integrity with explicit-solvent GROMACS MD (amber99sb-ildn, TIP3P) and AutoDock Vina ligand docking before shortlisting five mutants per enzyme.","dataviz":"The wiki is a server-rendered-feel React site with a genuinely engineered visual layer: a WebGL/Three.js distillation-tower scene and animated pathway loop on the landing page, every equation rendered as its own labelled SVG so the derivation reads as a chain, and predictor-agreement scatter plots (Figure 9) that shade the quadrant where FoldX and ThermoMPNN both call a substitution stabilising and enlarge it to label the five mutations actually sent to the bench. The Human Practices page is built as a clickable stacked column where each band is one of seven kinds of conversation across 21 entries, so a judge can jump straight to the stakeholder group they care about. Every figure carries a long descriptive alt attribute that states the figure's actual numbers, not just its subject.","datascience":"The headline data-science contribution is turning the protein engineering loop into a measured, auditable artefact. The team built a six-arm evaluation suite for Cofactor with four ablation regimes (bare, bare_prompt, tools, full) that add exactly one harness layer each, then scored outcomes: 37 deterministic probes in Arms A and B all pass, Arm C recorded zero workflow violations across 27 scorable runs under the phase machine versus two in the bare condition, and Arm D found the ungrounded condition hallucinated amino-acid positions while both grounded conditions did not. Arm E asked the agent to reconstruct the initial experimental designs of six published papers (three postdating the model's knowledge cutoff) and audited tool transcripts for contamination. On the wet-lab side, the promoter library was quantified as log-normalized mScarlet-i fluorescence per OD600 corrected for media background and P. putida autofluorescence, which revealed only three distinct expression tiers rather than the four reported in the literature.","software":"Cofactor, an agentic protein engineering platform released under MIT licence at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uottawa/cofactor. It wraps a general-purpose LLM in an explicit phase state machine whose preconditions are checked against a 16-table SQLite store (.cofactor/research.sqlite3, WAL journaling, foreign keys) rather than against the model's claims, so runs resume across sessions and the research record stays readable without the tool. A deterministic tool layer covers sequence operations, RFC10 compliance checking, codon optimisation, restriction analysis, solubility scanning, and retrieval from UniProt, NCBI, RCSB PDB, AlphaFold, PubMed and Europe PMC; an ExecutionManager submits jobs to local, SSH or Slurm backends. Three frontends ship: a FastAPI web UI, a Textual TUI and a CLI, all bring-your-own-key and model-agnostic against any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The team also released the P. putida handbook with protocols and the sanitized Golden Gate-ready pBBR1MCS-2 vector to the iGEM Registry.","stack":["COBRApy","GLPK","iJN1463 genome-scale model","FoldX","ThermoMPNN","ProteinMPNN embeddings","ConSurf","GROMACS","amber99sb-ildn / TIP3P","AutoDock Vina","Python 3.11","SQLite (WAL)","FastAPI","Textual TUI","Slurm / SSH job submission","React","Three.js / WebGL","Open Babel"],"techniques":["Flux balance analysis","Genome-scale metabolic model extension","Hinze-Kolmogorov droplet-breakup scaling","Film-theory interfacial mass transfer","Micellar solubilization modelling","Michaelis-Menten uptake kinetics","Bisection root-finding","Sensitivity analysis over droplet size","ddG ensemble prediction","Molecular dynamics (RMSF, native-contact persistence)","Ligand docking","LLM agent harness with phase-gated state machine","Ablation-based evaluation (4 regimes, 6 arms)","Rubric scoring by two independent judges","Techno-economic analysis","Life cycle assessment","HAZOP / P&ID process safety analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education is organised around four stated principles - Access, Adapt, Engage, Extend - and each initiative is written up against one of them. The team ran bilingual (English/French) sessions for 370+ children aged 4-13 in the Ottawa-Carleton District School Board's International and Indigenous Languages Program summer school, reached 70 more through the Ottawa Catholic School Board, and visited AcadeCap International School for ages 4-7. Original hands-on activities include Lava Lamps (oil properties), The Art of Consortia (a collaborative mural standing in for microbial division of labour), Mix and Match Genes, The Power of Bacterial DNA, and a Heavy vs Light Oil worksheet. For durability they published a free interactive children's magazine, Bio-Builder, distributed through local Ottawa public libraries explicitly to reach low-income families who cannot buy such resources, plus a demo list, infographics, a science and research podcast, and a full downloadable resource library of presentations and worksheets so the activities run without the team present. They also hosted the Great Canadian iGEM Incubator poster competition (with QGEM) and the Great Canadian Meetup pitch competition, both with published scoring rubrics.","ihp":"21 conversations across seven categories, each logged in the same three-part structure: why we went, what we heard, what we changed. The changes are concrete. Dr. Jinwen Chen (Natural Resources Canada, CanmetENERGY-Devon) and Dr. Stephen Larter (Calgary, petroleum geochemist) both told the team to abandon heavy diluted bitumen for diesel because of viscosity, mass-transfer and emulsion-separation barriers, and because diesel carries a hard <10 ppm sulfur limit and concentrated refractory DBTs - the team moved its entire target stream to diesel polishing. Dr. Rasha Maal-Bared warned that viscous crude mixes in localised laminar flow, which put static mixing elements and bioreactor sizing into the continuous-flow design. Patrick Lumley (Safe Sulphur Systems, 33 years in sulfur recovery) said the aqueous sulfite byproduct needs pH control to prevent SO2 release and that Claus-plant load reduction is economically worthless - so a pH control loop went into the P&ID and the business case was rebuilt around hydrogen savings and reduced hydrotreating severity instead of sulfur throughput. Larter's warning about microbially influenced corrosion added corrosion and material-compatibility criteria to the reactor design. Faculty of Engineering Design Day feedback forced the pivot from a full HDS-replacement narrative to the complementary polishing model. Dr. Corrie DaCosta, a protein chemist, was consulted specifically about what Cofactor's output report must contain to be usable by the person doing the engineering, and the report format changed as a result.","realWorld":"A fully worked commercialization case. Market sizing: TAM ~$3.1B (global HDS catalyst market), SAM ~$66M (Canada's 2.2% share, 16 refineries, ~2M bbl/day), SOM ~$1.4M from a single-refinery side-stream demo at a $0.40/bbl royalty on a 10,000 bbl/day stream. Revenue model is per-barrel licensing royalties, validated in conversation with an operations and project engineer. A techno-economic analysis compares BDS against HDS CAPEX of $16-42M USD for a 20,000-25,000 bbl/day refinery and projects 50-60% CAPEX reduction from eliminating high-pressure hydrogen hardware; a life cycle assessment with a declared system boundary and BDS/HDS inventories backs the environmental claim rather than asserting it. A staged timeline runs from proof of concept (2026) through publication and NSERC Alliance / Mitacs Accelerate / CRIN non-dilutive funding (2027), pilot demonstration on real feedstock plus formal pre-engagement with Environment and Climate Change Canada under New Substances Notification regulations for contained LMO deployment (2028-29), to licensing (2030+) and expansion into biodenitrogenation. The team deliberately targets smaller, older refineries facing Euro V limits with constrained hydrogen capacity rather than major oil sands operators, on Lumley's advice, and flags biogas producers as a faster early-revenue path. Risk analysis, SWOT, Business Model Canvas and a competitive positioning matrix are all published, and the team is explicit that Nulfur is harm reduction during the energy transition - \"a bridge, not a destination\".","partners":["Natural Resources Canada (CanmetENERGY-Devon)","Environment and Climate Change Canada, Low Carbon Fuels Division","Safe Sulphur Systems Ltd.","CDM Smith","Spanish National Research Council (CSIC), Systems Biology Program","Bedford Institute of Oceanography, Fisheries and Oceans Canada","Institute for Oil Sands Innovation / University of Alberta","University of Calgary","Queen's University","Arabian Gulf University","Hawk & Squirrel Innovation Inc.","Ottawa-Carleton District School Board (IILP Summer Program)","Ottawa Catholic School Board","AcadeCap International School","Ottawa Public Libraries","uOttawa eHub RevUp Circuit","MindFuel Tech Futures Challenge","QGEM (Great Canadian iGEM Incubator)","Clean Resource Innovation Network (CRIN)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/protein-engineering/fig-3.svg","caption":"Predictor-agreement scatter for 437 scored DszB mutations: the shaded quadrant is where FoldX and ThermoMPNN both call a substitution stabilising, enlarged to label the five variants advanced to the wet lab.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/cofactor/figures/fig-4b.avif","caption":"The Cofactor GUI - a local FastAPI web interface over the agentic protein engineering platform, showing chat, hypothesis and evidence views, job tracking and artifact browsing.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/consortia/fig-3.avif","caption":"Rate gain in DBT uptake from interfacial-area enhancement and micellar solubilization in isolation and combined; the shaded region is the synergy in excess of the product of the two isolated effects.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/education/adapt/art-consortia/pxl-20260716-173536329-mp.avif","caption":"The Art of Consortia activity in the Ottawa-Carleton District School Board summer language programme, where children build a shared mural to learn how a microbial consortium divides labour.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/process/igem-p-id-final.avif","caption":"Process and instrumentation diagram for the full-scale biodesulfurization plant, including the pH control loop added after Patrick Lumley warned that uncontrolled sulfite pH would release SO2.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy uOttawa's human-practices template literally: a single page where every one of 21 stakeholder conversations is logged in the same three fields - why we went, what we heard, what we changed - with the change written as a specific design edit (a pH loop in the P&ID, a switch of feedstock, static mixers). It costs nothing to adopt, makes a student team's reasoning auditable, and is the clearest way a school research centre can show that outside input actually moved the project rather than decorating it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/alternative-platform","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/process-design-and-analysis","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/gcm","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uottawa/cofactor"],"rank":110},{"team":"UPF-Barcelona","id":"6078","section":"Undergrad","country":"Spain","village":"Food & Agriculture","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/","projectTitle":"Vinesys","tagline":"Where the rain runs out, the roots hold on: engineering root-associated Bacillus subtilis to hold water around grapevine roots.","summary":"Vinesys engineers Bacillus subtilis to colonise the grapevine rhizosphere and overexpress gamma-PGA, a water-retaining biopolymer, plus ACC deaminase to trim the drought-stress hormone precursor ACC, forming a thin hydration shell where the bacterium attaches to the root. Drought is detected by a two-layer switch: an osmolarity-responsive promoter plus a salt-dependent intein (HutMCM2) that the team re-engineered in silico to splice at the roughly 600-725 mM intracellular K+ that B. subtilis actually reaches, rather than its native 1.5 M. The dry lab produced three distinct deliverables: RK4 solver, a standalone no-code genetic-circuit designer and simulator; a documented molecular-dynamics pipeline for tuning salt-dependent proteins; and iGEMforge, a Claude Code plugin of four research skills that they evaluated honestly and then declined to use for project decisions. Awards came for Software, Model and Education.","awards":["Best Software — Winner (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"RK4 solver is a desktop Python application with a GUI built in Dear PyGui, structured as three partially independent modules (GUI, compiler, solver) so the solver can be driven from Python without drawing anything on screen. It ships for Windows, macOS and Linux. Interchange is handled by two formats: a readable personal JSON format and SBML Level 3, which gives round-trip compatibility with COPASI and Tellurium. The molecular-dynamics pipeline runs ColabFold/AlphaFold for structures, FoldX for stability screening, MAFFT and BLAST for candidate selection, UCSF Chimera for inspection, and OpenMM with Amber force fields (tleap setup) for the simulations. iGEMforge is a Claude Code plugin: four Markdown SKILL files plus a couple of Python scripts in a folder, deliberately with no subagents, hooks or custom MCP servers.","dataviz":"RK4 solver's results screen plots species concentrations while every parameter has a live slider that takes effect during the running simulation, which is the core interaction idea. The editor uses blocks whose icons follow SBOL Visual glyphs, with input pins always on the left and output pins on the right so links read left to right, collapsible blocks, and a diagnostics/warnings pane with connection rules that refuse to let you build an invalid circuit. The wiki itself is built as explanatory interactive JavaScript rather than static figures: an in-browser Euler-vs-RK4 comparison on x'' = -x, a step-through animation of the solver's operator stack, a replayable eval harness showing the same prompt answered with and without an iGEMforge skill, and a hoverable stakeholder map on the entrepreneurship page.","datascience":"The solver was validated numerically against COPASI: three example circuits were exported to SBML and re-simulated in COPASI under LSODA with identical parameters, initial concentrations and simulation time, and every quantity that means the same thing in both tools agreed within 0.21%, most within 0.01%. The apparent 13.4% and 98.5% discrepancies on free mazF and mazE were traced to a labelling difference in rapid-equilibrium binding, not integration error, and resolved by comparing totals (mazF + 2*FEF, mazE + FEF), which then matched within 0.01% and 0.09%. iGEMforge was evaluated in two rounds: 11 hand-graded assertion tests in round one, then 28 cases run three times per arm (with-skill vs. without-skill) using Claude Code's `claude plugin eval` with an LLM-as-a-judge against team-written rubrics, including real cases, synthetic cases with deliberately planted flaws, and 'silence' cases where a skill should stay out of the way. The MD work used replicas, a wild-type positive control at its known 1.5 M threshold, an uncharged no-effect control, and re-ran its analysis under several different attack-distance cutoffs to show the conclusion did not depend on one arbitrary value.","software":"RK4 solver is the main tool: a free, open-source, cross-platform application that turns a hand-drawn genetic circuit into a running ODE simulation with no code at all, aimed squarely at iGEM teams, high-school and undergraduate students, and explicitly not at replacing COPASI or SimBiology. Eight block types are implemented (production with Hill activation/repression, degradation, complex formation in rapid-equilibrium or kinetic mode, extracellular species with six dosing regimes, cell entry, export, catalysis with Michaelis-Menten or mass action, and a link probe), and the compiler translates the drawing into a flat list of stack operators which the solver evaluates with Euler or 4th-order Runge-Kutta. The architecture is worth copying: the translator sorts each block's contribution by the species it points to rather than by the block's own species, a redesign forced by discovering that complex formation was writing its negative terms into the wrong derivative. It was validated twice, against COPASI numerically and against the team's own biocontainment toxin-antitoxin model biologically, and it was user-tested, with non-expert testers building and tuning a toggle switch in 5 to 20 minutes after a short introduction to the notation. Limitations are stated plainly on the wiki (deterministic only, partial SBML import, custom block unfinished, GUI not yet as intuitive as they want) with a three-horizon roadmap including SBOL-based integration with the iGEM Registry that would pull part parameters in and mark any user override as traceably overwritten. Source: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/upf-barcelona/upf-software (team repo https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/upf-barcelona). It is distributed as a downloadable cross-platform application, not a hosted web service, so there is no click-and-use URL. The second tool, iGEMforge, is the most interesting negative result of the season: a Claude Code plugin of four skills (scientific-evidence, experimental-design, mathematical-modeling, bio-wiki-animations) built over two DBTL cycles, in which round-one hand-grading suggested the skills helped (bio-wiki-animations 12/13 assertions with the skill against 5/12 without) but round two with three runs per arm and an LLM judge showed no clear average gain (experimental-design -0.11, scientific-evidence +0.04, mathematical-modeling no clear difference), at substantially higher token cost (309k vs 224k; 468k vs 201k). Testing revealed that three of four skills intermittently stalled waiting on user confirmation and returned nothing; cycle two fixed it with a single rule written into the skills ('state the assumption or caveat and continue'), which restored output in 3 of 3 runs but still did not beat the base model. The team's conclusion, published on the wiki, is that the evidence was too thin to use iGEMforge for Vinesys decisions, and they list cost, privacy, over-trust and installation risks explicitly.","stack":["Python","Dear PyGui","SBML Level 3","SBOL Visual","JSON","COPASI (validation target)","Tellurium","OpenMM","Amber force field / tleap","ColabFold","AlphaFold","FoldX","MAFFT","BLAST","UCSF Chimera","Claude Code plugin (Markdown skills + Python)","claude plugin eval","iGEM GitLab","JavaScript (interactive wiki figures)"],"techniques":["ODE modelling of genetic circuits","4th-order Runge-Kutta and Euler integration","stack-machine operator compilation of differential equations","Hill activation/repression kinetics","rapid-equilibrium and kinetic complex formation","Michaelis-Menten catalysis","molecular dynamics with near-attack-conformation (NAC) readout","ddG stability screening","pLDDT/PAE structure confidence assessment","LLM-as-a-judge evaluation with pre-written rubrics","A/B testing with planted-flaw synthetic cases","SBML round-trip cross-validation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education is organised around an explicit six-step method the team named WONDER (Who, Objectives, Notice, Dialogue, Evidence, Record), whose last step feeds back into the first like a DBTL cycle, and which is rooted in Catalan educacio del lleure (leisure education) practice that several team members came from through scouting. Audiences were plotted on two axes, age and prior synbio interest, and the session language (Catalan, Spanish or English) was chosen from the barrier they wanted to lower. Each session required a before-and-after measure, at least one artefact participants made themselves, a rubric completed within 24 hours, and a written summary within a day covering what actually happened and what they would change, published on the wiki so other teams can run the session without them. Activities spanned a MiniGEM where pre-university students designed their own synthetic biology projects, an escape room at CosmoCaixa, La Nit de la Recerca, the UPF Summer School, a role-play at the PRBB, SynBYSS, Centre Civic Guinardo, scout groups (Cau La Floresta) and several schools, plus a round table with iGEM UB.","ihp":"Human practices is written as three acts with a stated principle of intentionality: every design choice should have a reason statable to an outsider, and where there is none they say so. 32 people outside the team across 8 countries, 52 documented exchanges and 103 survey respondents, with 9 of 10 project phases opened to outside input. The first grower they met corrected their founding premise, that drought was killing Catalan vineyards, by explaining that summer water stress is necessary for proper ripening and the real damage is to next season's crop; a second grower said vines did die in the first drought year, and the team kept both rather than reconciling them. A gamma-PGA geneticist in Pavia shrank the central claim from bulk soil water retention to a local hydration shell at the root. A Bordeaux researcher set a condition the design cannot meet (no deficit at flowering, moderate deficit later) and the team publishes it as a limit, not a feature. Two further conventions are worth copying: advice they chose not to follow stays on the page with the reason, and disagreements they could not resolve are printed unresolved. They also disclose that one advisor, Dr. Carolina Elizondo, holds an iGEM role, so readers can weigh her input.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship page is explicit that the current value is knowledge, education and visibility rather than product sales, with the project at contained laboratory proof of concept and no environmental release. More than 50 organisations were contacted since June 2026; wineries Clos Montblanc (DO Conca de Barberà), Sumarroca and Vallformosa (DO Penedès) were interviewed about how a new input would fit existing practice, with Vallformosa noting that acting at root level could mean lower adoption friction. IRTA, the Catalan government's agrifood research institute, reviewed the approach against its own Penedès soil-and-water initiative and provided winery contacts. The regulatory finding was that certification, not GMO status, is the binding constraint, and the business work reached a cost per hectare and a route to a field trial. Scale context: 52,000 ha of Catalan vineyard, 8,500 viticulturists, EUR 3.27 bn annual turnover, reservoirs at 14.3% in 2024.","partners":["IRTA (Institut de Recerca i Tecnologia Agroalimentàries)","Celler Clos Montblanc","Sumarroca","Vallformosa","Ajuntament de Barcelona (Departament de Ciència i Innovació)","CosmoCaixa","PRBB","iGEM UB","Anthropic (sponsor)","University of Pavia (gamma-PGA genetics)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure6.avif","caption":"Screenshot of the RK4 solver editor with the biocontainment toxin-antitoxin circuit built from linked blocks.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure7.avif","caption":"RK4 solver output: internal resveratrol, mazF and FEF levels over time under sinusoidal resveratrol production.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure4.avif","caption":"The first hand-drawn sketches of the RK4 solver blocks, designed in the style of electronic circuit diagrams.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Validate a simulator against the established tool in the field and publish the discrepancy table, including the one that looked like a 98% error and turned out to be a labelling difference, because that table is what makes a student-built solver trustworthy. Their AI work is the sharper lesson for a research centre: build the agent plugin, then actually A/B evaluate it with planted-flaw cases and an LLM judge, and be willing to publish that it did not beat the base model and that you therefore did not use it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/igem-forge","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/dry-tips","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/description","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/upf-barcelona/upf-software"],"rank":26},{"team":"BOKU-Vienna","id":"6090","section":"Overgrad","country":"Austria","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/","projectTitle":"Algaeneering","tagline":"Engineering the microalga Chlamydomonas reinhardtii into a vitamin C and folate factory to fight hidden hunger","summary":"BOKU-Vienna overexpressed GGP (vitamin C) and GTPCHI plus ADCS (folate) in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii using Golden Gate Level 0/1/2 assembly, reaching a 4.5x increase in vitamin C in the GGP strain. The project was framed around hidden hunger, which affects an estimated two billion people, and was linked to UN SDGs 2, 3 and 12. Its education practice was built on an explicit didactic framework (the Didactic Triangle) and spanned four goals: sparking curiosity, classroom science, public dialogue and educational media. A genome-scale metabolic model was coupled to a calibrated bioreactor model and a nutritional impact model to translate lab titres into how many people a production plant could feed.","awards":["Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Genome-scale metabolic modelling of C. reinhardtii (iCre1355, mixotrophic version) with flux balance analysis (FBA), flux variability analysis (FVA), in-silico knockouts and enzyme protein-cost ranking; dynamic FBA bioreactor model calibrated and validated against the team's own flask cultures; a nutritional impact model converting measured vitamin content into daily requirements and people supplied.","dataviz":"Interactive 'Try the trade-off' widget on the wiki: sliders let a visitor pick a vitamin and see (left) maximum vitamin flux versus growth rate from the genome-scale model and (right) the calibrated flask culture's biomass, ammonium, acetate and vitamin titre over up to 120 h, with the team's own measured points overlaid and a harvest-point marker. Each graph carries an explicit 'How to read this graph' panel and a parameter-provenance table.","datascience":"Lab-to-model feedback loop: flask measurements (biomass, ammonium, acetate, vitamin titre) were used to calibrate model parameters, and the model's pre-registered 1-10x overexpression band was compared against the measured 4.5x vitamin C result. Known model gaps were stated openly (measured ammonium ~2 mM below model; no cell-death term).","software":"Custom interactive children's learning platform (web app, guided by an algae mascot, virtual-microscope zoom from cell to molecule, 3D DNA double helix with VR headset support, base-pairing puzzle game) and a separate 13-module self-paced interactive learning platform with mini-games (lac operon switch, PCR exponential-growth slider, mRNA translation, Cas9 cut-site finder), instant feedback, no sign-up, and a teacher preview mode that unlocks all modules for classroom prep.","stack":["genome-scale metabolic model iCre1355","flux balance analysis","flux variability analysis","dynamic FBA","web-based interactive learning platform","WebVR / 3D DNA model","Kahoot","Mentimeter / QR-code live surveys"],"techniques":["Golden Gate Assembly (Level 0 to Level 2)","C. reinhardtii transformation (UVM4)","vitamin C and folate assays","microscopy","protein-cost analysis of enzyme targets","bioreactor scale-up scenario modelling"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"BOKU-Vienna ran a four-pillar education strategy explicitly grounded in the Didactic Triangle (learner-educator-content), and designed every child-facing print and digital resource in the dyslexia-friendly ABeeZee font. Pillar 1 (sparking curiosity) included a three-day Children's University (KinderUNI) booth built as a six-station progressive learning path: project poster, live Chlorella tubes and Chlamydomonas microscope images, a handcrafted 3D algae organelle model, a custom web learning platform plus a VR headset for exploring a 3D DNA double helix, an interactive genetic-engineering poster, and a gamified 'plasmid toss' into a large algae model to represent transformation; gummy-bear DNA building ran alongside. The same station set was then carried to Karwan Haus, a Caritas Wien refugee shelter in Vienna, on 17 August 2026 for children aged 4-12, with the flyer translated into four languages, Arabic- and Russian-speaking team members explaining stations, illustration-heavy materials, child-sized lab coats and goggles lent by KinderUNI BOKU, Lego-brick Golden Gate Cloning, stamps and science badges; in the exit survey most children chose 'I would love to work in science in the future'. Pillar 2 put a three-week, four-visit curriculum into two primary schools (Ruckergasse in Vienna and Neuarzl in Tyrol) with strawberry DNA extraction, lab-safety and pipetting sessions, a construct-assembly exercise for the GGP enzyme, vitamin C detection, Kahoot recaps and a field trip to the ZOOM Children's Museum where pupils wrote, drew and voiced two stop-motion films about algae and genetic modification; a middle-school workshop ('Code of Life - How We Program Cells') added a red-cabbage anthocyanin biosensor experiment, and a high-school visit to BG/BRG Tulln had students identify C. reinhardtii against Spirulina and Chlorella under the microscope. Measured impact: in the primary-school pre/post evaluation, zero children named science as a desired profession at the start versus five at the end, and 75.0% said they had become curious about programming cells (16.7% wanted to learn more first, 8.3% said they definitely want to do it in future). Pillar 3 (public dialogue) comprised a booth at Austria's Long Night of Research (24 April 2026) with a live visitor survey board on GMO acceptance and awareness that over 5 billion people face micronutrient deficiency, the Muthgassen Summer Festival, a 'Future of Food' panel discussion at TU Wien's Boecklsaal on 3 October 2026 with about 60 attendees and four panelists spanning algae cosmetics (Rita Mota), UNICEF Austria malnutrition work (Michael Blauensteiner), algae production engineering (Lukas Neuwirth, ex-Ecoduna) and microalgae research (Johannes Stadlmann), run in three blocks with anonymous QR-code audience polling and spirulina cotton candy, plus a pub quiz for about thirty STEM and non-STEM students. Pillar 4 produced durable media: an illustrated German children's book 'Welche Sprache spricht das Leben?' co-developed with KinderBOKU (characters such as 'Grandma Polymerase' who knits DNA together), launched at Vienna's Pappenheimgasse public library for a 4th-grade and a 5th-grade class with a copy donated to the library's lending collection and a copy given to every child; a comic advised by Martina Froehlich of KinderBOKU; the German-language podcast 'AlgaeHearing' on Spotify, deliberately in German because accessible German science content is scarce; the 13-module interactive learning platform with a teacher preview mode; and a feature article in the German-language life-science magazine BioSpektrum (issue 32, 4 June 2026). Critically, all tested worksheets, workshop guides and experiment protocols were published as downloadable Open Educational Resources on eduvidual.at, the official learning platform of the Austrian Federal Ministry of Education, Science and Research, so Austrian teachers nationwide can reuse them. Named-attribution testimonials were collected from the teacher at Ruckergasse, the head of the Pappenheimgasse library, the head of KinderBOKU and a social worker at Karwan Haus.","ihp":"Human Practices was structured into three sequential phases, each with its own stakeholder set: (1) lab experts for technical feasibility, which changed key design decisions; (2) outside critics on GMOs, sustainability and ethics, where the team explicitly sought disconfirming rather than validating views; (3) nutrition and product-design conversations about who should be prioritised as a target group and how the product would be delivered. The team framed iHP against the reflective/responsible/responsive triad, mapped the project to SDGs 2, 3 and 12, and used the 'Future of Food' panel plus audience polling as a live iHP instrument. The panel's main takeaway was recorded honestly as 'our work is meaningful, but GMO-based projects are not without risk and open questions, especially when implemented at a larger scale'.","realWorld":"The impact model translated measured vitamin content into concrete figures: 25 g dry algae per day covers one daily folate requirement and 116 g covers vitamin C; a hypothetical 100 m3 production plant at wild-type folate content could cover the yearly folate need of roughly 900 adults or 490 pregnant women. The design reactor scenario produced 0.46 g/L/day, nitrogen-capped, at about 1.5x the flask vitamin titre. The team also published a separate 'iGEM guide' page for teams starting out.","partners":["KinderBOKU / KinderUNI BOKU (BOKU University children's science programme)","Caritas Wien - Karwan Haus refugee shelter","ZOOM Children's Museum, Vienna","Pappenheimgasse public library, Vienna","Ruckergasse Primary School, Vienna","Neuarzl Primary School, Tyrol","BG/BRG 21 bilingual middle school","BG/BRG Tulln high school","TU Wien (Boecklsaal venue)","eduvidual.at - Austrian Federal Ministry of Education, Science and Research learning platform","BioSpektrum magazine","UNICEF Austria (panelist)","Stroeck (sponsor, panel catering)","Polymun"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/kinderuni/kinderuni-02.avif","caption":"Children at the Children's University (KinderUNI) six-station synthetic biology booth in Vienna","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/karwan-haus/karwan-haus-golden-gate-assembly-via-lego-bricks.avif","caption":"Teaching Golden Gate assembly with Lego bricks at Karwan Haus, a Caritas Wien refugee shelter","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/childrensbook/childrensbook-front.avif","caption":"The team's illustrated German children's book, co-developed with KinderBOKU and donated to a Vienna public library","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/lernplattform/1-overview-of-our-interactive-learning-platform-with-13-modules.avif","caption":"The free 13-module interactive learning platform, with no sign-up and a teacher preview mode","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/panel-discussion/panel-discussion-people.avif","caption":"The 'Future of Food' public panel discussion at TU Wien, about 60 attendees with live QR-code polling","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/primary-school/primary-school-02.avif","caption":"Pupils extracting DNA during the three-week primary-school curriculum","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Publish every tested worksheet, workshop guide and experiment protocol as open educational resources on the national ministry education platform (as BOKU did on eduvidual.at), so school use continues after the project ends, and measure the same cohort before and after (career aspirations plus curiosity items) so learning gain is a number, not an anecdote.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/model"],"rank":7},{"team":"XJTLU-China","id":"6096","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/","projectTitle":"dogmaOS — programming translation rhythm for soluble protein expression","tagline":"Design where and for how long the ribosome should pause, then re-engineer the host tRNA pool to make that pause possible.","summary":"XJTLU-China asked whether soluble yield of hard-to-express recombinant proteins in E. coli can be improved by deliberately programming *where* translation slows down, rather than by maximising expression. Their dry-lab system catmaOS (Codon-Aware Translation Modeling and Analysis Operating System) chains four models: structural co-translational folding priority (M1), evolutionary conserved slow-codon evidence (M2), a five-layer translation-kinetics and ribosome-traffic engine with constrained Pareto selection (M3-A to M3-E), and host tRNA-pool intervention design (M4). The framework picked a single synonymous G-CSF design (W1-S) out of six candidates and an engineered BL21-AlaPro chassis, both of which went to the bench; Four-Leaf Clover RT-qPCR then measured the actual tRNA knockdown and that number was fed back into M3-A as an updated host context. It won Best Model (Undergraduate) and Best Education (Undergraduate).","awards":["Best Measurement — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Model — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Education — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"catmaOS is a four-model, five-layer multiscale pipeline and is unusually explicit about what each layer is and is not. M1 is a structural-bioinformatics scoring model: it builds a residue-level evidence table from PDB coordinates (G-CSF, PDB 1RHG chain B, UniProt P09919, RefSeq NM_000759.4), runs DSSP for secondary structure and relative solvent accessibility, solves a self-consistent native-contact occupancy model as chain length grows (Jacobs-Shakhnovich) to detect folding events at a 1 kBT free-energy-drop threshold with a 4 Angstrom contact cutoff, adds Kyte-Doolittle hydrophobic exposure, cysteine-neighbourhood scores, DSSP transition and beta-initiation tracks and an AGGRESCAN-style aggregation propensity, then fuses them with availability-aware weights into a 0-100 priority score and shifts it downstream by a 35-codon ribosome exit-tunnel offset (scanned 30-40 codons for sensitivity). M2 is a comparative-genomics model: MAFFT L-INS-i protein alignment projected back to codons with PAL2NAL, GtRNAdb/tRNAscan-SE high-confidence tRNA gene copy numbers per species, a frozen codon-anticodon wobble-penalty table, a tRNA-adaptation-style effective supply W_s(c) converted to within-species relative 'resistance', a top-25%-resistance slow-codon label, a strict same-amino-acid filter, and Henikoff position-based sequence weighting to produce a weighted slow-codon conservation score with an explicit effective-coverage track. M3 is the kinetic core: M3-A decomposes intrinsic codon dwell into decoding (inverse effective cognate/wobble supply with a near-cognate competition term, K_dec = 0.80, gamma_near = 0.50), peptidyl-transfer (proline/EF-P arrest context), basal translocation and ViennaRNA RNAplfold local-unpaired-probability RNA resistance, with four ablation configurations A0-A3 to attribute mechanism; M3-B feeds that rate vector into TWO independent ribosome-traffic backends — a deterministic Ribosome Flow Model with Extended Objects (RFMEO) steady state and a stochastic Gillespie kinetic Monte Carlo extended-object TASEP (5 replicates, SEM bands, warm-up discarded) — and cross-validates them with Pearson, Spearman, concordance and top-k hotspot overlap plus an alpha-beta boundary-sensitivity grid; M3-C maps traffic-safe dwell envelopes, exit-tunnel availability gates, relative contact-order folding demand, a finite-state productive-pathway propagator p_{L+1} = p_L exp(s Q_L dt_L) run over a 0.5/1/2 scale grid (direction called 'robust' only when the sign holds across scales), and a time-weighted unsatisfied-native-contact exposure proxy E_U = sum_L U(L) tau(L); M3-D closes a first-order steady-state mRNA balance M_ss = k_tx / (k_deg,eff + mu) with a scikit-learn mRNA-decay predictor trained on 5,486 curated E. coli half-life records (Chen 2015, Selinger 2003) split gene-grouped 3,883/765/838; M3-E generates synonymous candidates under reading-frame, GC, motif, restriction-site and homopolymer constraints and selects by constrained Pareto dominance over four frozen objectives (maximise terminal flux J, minimise ribosome load R, maximise productive-pathway C4, minimise exposure C5), with exact enumeration for small spaces and NSGA-II for large ones, scored for precision/recall against exact fronts. M4 extends design to the host: it maps target codons to BL21 tRNA genes, clusters and promoters with a weighted K-12-to-BL21 transfer score (S_map = 0.45 S_signature + 0.20 S_ordered + 0.15 S_count + 0.15 I_80 + 0.05 I_200), proposes copy-number reduction or 1-3 substitutions in a 29-bp promoter core against 15/30/50% weakening tiers, and ranks them with a three-model ML ensemble trained on 112,955 measured core promoters from the published MBRS L4 library (Zhou 2025), held out by -35/-10 motif pairs (67,978/7,680/37,297) achieving r = 0.677, MAE = 0.506 on log2(1+strength).","dataviz":"Eighteen numbered model figures that read as a single argument. Aligned multi-track profiles put six normalised structural feature tracks (F1-F6) above the availability-aware composite score so a reader can see which feature drove a peak; priority profiles are paired with an evidence-coverage track underneath so a high score with thin evidence cannot be misread as a finding. Figure 8 is a tunnel-length sensitivity heatmap (rows = assumed exit-tunnel length 30-40 codons, columns = codon position, fixed 0-100 teal scale, light-grey cells for genuinely unavailable data rather than zero) so the reader can watch high-priority bands migrate as an assumption changes. Figure 11 is a codon-level audit matrix (18 homolog rows x codons 110-121, green = slow in that species' tRNA context, white = comparable, grey = non-comparable). Figure 14 is a log2(candidate/WT) dwell heatmap restricted to edited positions only, with the x-axis explicitly flagged as non-continuous and the legend explaining that +1 means 2x. Figure 16 is a three-panel dot plot of WT-normalised flux, ribosome load and exposure on matched candidate rows, deliberately NOT collapsed into one score. KMC traces carry +/- SEM bands and separate unblocked residence from blocked waiting. Captions state the direction of preference and whether a quantity is measured or a relative model proxy.","datascience":"Two supervised models were trained from public measurement data and one from their own: an mRNA degradation proxy (D2) on 5,486 curated E. coli half-life records with gene/protein/CDS-grouped splits, a 29-bp promoter-strength ensemble on 112,955 measured core promoters held out by -35/-10 motif pairs (a genuinely out-of-distribution split, r = 0.677), and a codon-occupancy regression on 107,627 codon-level Ribo-seq observations across 125 genes with a documented 14-nt P-site offset, first/last 10 codons excluded, and gene-held-out evaluation (22,025 test observations). The team audits its own provenance in public: several dataset source fields are annotated 'unconfirmed', 'remains to be confirmed' or 'not an attributed published dataset size' rather than quietly cited. Model 4's proL promoter screen predicted 0.727 (native) vs 0.386/0.366/0.358 (P1-P3); the built strain BL21-AlaPro was then measured by Four-Leaf Clover RT-qPCR, showing rpoD-normalised mature tRNA falling from 63.98 to 23.42 (alaXW) and 6.828 to 2.254 (proL), and those measured numbers were returned to M3-A as a new effective-supply table for a full A-E rerun. The headline wet-lab readout: TolB-G-CSF model-designed sequence in the engineered host reached a mean soluble/insoluble ratio of 0.140 vs 0.075 for the commercial sequence in WT host (DnaK-normalised, n = 3 biological replicates, ~1.87-fold). Crucially the team also reports the negative result honestly — for PfCHT1 and leptin, insoluble bands weakened but no clear soluble enrichment appeared, and the results figure is literally titled 'Solubility Gains Remain Unclear'.","software":"catmaOS 4.02.06 ships as a downloadable Windows x64 source release with Setup.cmd/Start.cmd, a pinned requirements-lock.txt, a private .venv, a local browser workspace on 127.0.0.1:8767 (sequences never leave the user's machine), a 28-page PDF user manual, and four precomputed read-only interactive demos embedded in the wiki with JSON/CSV export. Every run emits a reproducibility bundle: request.json (typed input, label, source, parameters), result.json on a versioned schema 1.0.0 with input hash, module status and reproducibility record, curves.csv, metrics.csv, exact_candidates.csv, native/evidence-result.json and run.log, all zipped per run_id. The design rule stated on the page — 'missing values stay missing' and 'a completed run confirms the requested task finished; it does not imply every model was executed' — is the discipline most teams lack.","stack":["Python 3.14","NumPy","SciPy","pandas","Biopython","scikit-learn","Matplotlib","DSSP","MAFFT L-INS-i","PAL2NAL","ViennaRNA / RNAplfold","AGGRESCAN","GtRNAdb / tRNAscan-SE","NSGA-II","custom RFMEO solver","custom Gillespie KMC"],"techniques":["co-translational folding free-energy model","structural bioinformatics / DSSP feature fusion","exit-tunnel coordinate mapping with sensitivity scan","comparative genomics codon alignment","tRNA adaptation index-style supply model","Henikoff sequence weighting","ODE/steady-state ribosome flow model (RFMEO)","stochastic Gillespie kinetic Monte Carlo TASEP with extended objects","finite-state Markov folding-pathway propagation","steady-state mRNA mass balance","multi-objective constrained Pareto optimisation","NSGA-II genetic algorithm","machine-learning regression (promoter strength, mRNA decay, codon occupancy)","out-of-distribution held-out validation","ablation studies","backend cross-validation","parameter-scenario robustness analysis","model-to-measurement feedback loop"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Best Education winner (Undergraduate). The team built an explicit education framework rather than a list of events: a four-tier 'Lifelong Learning Pathway' (interest inspiration, knowledge building, interdisciplinary co-creation, professional impact) that replaces age-banding with depth of engagement, crossed with a four-stage activity structure (Reach, Construct, Dialogue, Extension) and Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences as the design lens for choosing an activity format. They reached over 2,000 people through 21 activities — workshops, exhibitions and the Learning Mall Premium online platform — and produced 18 reusable education toolkits, with the Extension stage deliberately designed so each activity leaves behind a game, handbook or toolkit rather than ending at the event.","ihp":"A CARE framework distilled from stakeholder conversations: Create Accessibility, Accountable Storytelling, Real-world Alignment, Ethical Design. It originated in a question from Dr Lanlan Han about who actually benefits and how strong the evidence is. The interview timeline is structured as Problem / Design / Scope, and the team documents how it narrowed its own claim: starting from 'boost heterologous protein expression in E. coli', literature and interviews with Dr Antony Kam and industry expert Dr Xijun Piao (CATUG Biotechnology, Suzhou) pushed them to redefine success as soluble usable protein rather than total expression, and to state explicitly that dogmaOS is 'not positioned as a universal protein-production platform'. Mid-stage consultations with Dr Yongtao Zhu, Dr Chun Chan, Prof Sheng Yang (Shanghai Institutes for Biological Sciences, CAS), Prof Zhiliang Lu, Dr Avinash Pandreka (Oxford Suzhou Centre for Advanced Research), Dr Kozo Hamada and Prof Zhongmei Liu (Jiangnan University) convinced them that translation speed alone could not explain folding outcomes, which is what created Model 3 and Model 4.","realWorld":"Target selection was driven by real demand: G-CSF, leptin, PfCHT1 and FGF2 were chosen with researchers and protein-production companies as proteins that are hard to express in E. coli and have clear research or clinical uses spanning public health, rare disease and basic research. The team maps the project to SDGs 3, 9, 12 and 17. Accessibility is the commercial thesis — rather than selling a service, catmaOS is released as free local software so academic labs and iGEM teams with only basic E. coli facilities can do upstream optimisation instead of paying for repeated trial-and-error. Safety is built in: non-pathogenic strains, BSL-1 practice, autoclaved waste, and FLP-FRT removal of the kanamycin cassette after lambda-Red genome editing.","partners":["Xi'an Jiaotong-Liverpool University","CATUG Biotechnology (Suzhou) Co., Ltd.","Shanghai Institutes for Biological Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Oxford Suzhou Centre for Advanced Research","Jiangnan University","GenScript","SnapGene"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-08.svg","caption":"Figure 8: exit-tunnel sensitivity heatmap. Rows are assumed ribosome exit-tunnel lengths of 30-40 codons, columns are G-CSF codon positions, and the fixed 0-100 teal scale shows structural priority. Light-grey cells mark genuinely unavailable structural data rather than a score of zero, so the reader can watch high-priority bands migrate as a modelling assumption changes.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-11.svg","caption":"Figure 11: codon-level audit matrix for reference positions 110-121. Each row is a retained homolog; green marks codons called slow in that species' own tRNA context, white marks other comparable synonymous codons, grey retains non-comparable cells. Positions 115 and 121 were slow in all 18 homologs while 118 was slow in none.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/merge-20261020/catmaos-figure-12.svg","caption":"Figure 12: the full 'how long to pause' panel, linking synonymous candidate generation to intrinsic dwell (M3-A), kinetic Monte Carlo ribosome traffic with SEM bands (M3-B), folding trajectories and contact exposure (M3-C), the mRNA decay proxy (M3-D) and the constrained objective screen (M3-E) in which four of six candidates pass the flux and ribosome-burden gates.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-16.svg","caption":"Figure 16: throughput, ribosome load and exposure trade-offs for wild type plus six synonymous candidates, shown as three WT-normalised panels on matched rows and deliberately not collapsed into a single score. W1-F had flux 0.781 and load 1.079, failing the flux >= 0.95 constraint; W1-S passed on all four objectives and went to the bench.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Pair every model score with an explicit evidence-coverage track and run a sensitivity scan over your least-defensible assumption (here, exit-tunnel length), so a figure shows not just the answer but how much of it is real data; and keep competing objectives as separate aligned panels rather than summing them into one invented score. Both are achievable with matplotlib by a motivated 16-year-old and immediately make a model honest.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/measurement/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/education/"],"rank":66},{"team":"Uni-Padua-IT","id":"6151","section":"Undergrad","country":"Italy","village":"Space","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/","projectTitle":"C.O.S.M.I.C. (Cyanobacterial Oxygen Supply for Martian Inhabitant Complexes)","tagline":"A Nostoc-based life-support chassis for Mars, plus ReTIsigner, an open codon optimiser for the 35,799 hosts other tools ignore.","summary":"Uni-Padua-IT engineered the filamentous cyanobacterium Nostoc sp. PCC 7120 as a Martian life-support organism: it fixes CO2 into breathable oxygen, degrades the perchlorates that poison Martian water (via native NarB and a heterocyst-compartmentalised pcrABD plus chlorite dismutase), and carries pprA from Deinococcus radiodurans for radiation-damage repair, behind a copper-dependent MazF kill switch. Because no free codon optimiser listed their chassis, they forked TIsigner into ReTIsigner, a frequency-weighted codon optimiser that reads any of the 35,799 Kazusa codon usage tables, enforces GC bounds, actively excises RFC restriction sites and reports CAI, GC and translation-initiation opening energy. ReTIsigner was validated in silico over 100 sequences and four hosts, then in vivo: a ReTIsigner-optimised eYFP gave a 2.16-fold common-effect increase in specific fluorescence across DH5-alpha and BL21 (p = 0.002, permutation test stratified by strain). They also built an open-source acoustic (piezo + Arduino) bubble sensor so oxygen production can be followed without opening a sealed culture.","awards":["Best Software — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Space Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"SvelteKit single-page frontend built from scratch, talking to a Flask backend over a single documented HTTP endpoint (POST /optimise); a worker pool behind Flask shells out to the compiled scientific binaries. The whole stack is shipped as a two-stage Docker image (Node.js 22 build stage; Miniconda runtime pinning Python 3.6.9, ViennaRNA 2.5.1 and Infernal 1.1.5) exposing port 5050.","dataviz":"Results view compares input against each optimised sequence in a results card showing CAI, GC content and opening energy, so sequences no longer have to be analysed one at a time in external tools. A built-in guided tour walks a first-time user through every field of the run configuration. Exports in three reproducible formats (CSV, PDF, FASTA), each recording the full run configuration plus the computed analyses. Validation figures (specific-fluorescence bar charts and ratio plots with 95% CIs) are published as SVG on the wiki, and the system is explained with two architecture diagrams: the POST /optimise request path and one iteration of the annealing loop.","datascience":"In silico benchmark over 100 coding sequences optimised for four hosts spanning roughly 31-75% GC, recording GC, CAI, opening energy and failure rate with and without a codon usage table: supplying a table cut the failure rate from 52% to 2% while leaving opening energy essentially unchanged (mean difference +/-0.06 kcal/mol). Wet-lab validation used independent cultures as the unit of replication, three cultures per sample each in technical triplicate, fluorescence compared at matched optical density rather than fixed time, two detection channels, and a permutation test stratified by strain; the team explicitly states the confidence interval (about 1.6-2.8x) and what the data does not license them to claim.","software":"ReTIsigner is a free, Apache 2.0-licensed codon optimiser for non-model hosts, and it is the clearest example this year of forking an existing scientific tool rather than rebuilding one. The team started from TIsigner (Bhandari, Lim & Gardner), contacted its authors for feedback on their proposal, and then added four things TIsigner lacked: frequency-weighted synonymous codon sampling driven by any Kazusa codon usage table (35,799 organisms) instead of one-amino-acid-one-codon, an optional GC bound enforced as a feasibility filter, active position-targeted removal of restriction sites with a re-check for newly introduced sites, and in-app CAI/GC analysis per output sequence. The restriction-site work is the headline engineering result: optimising perchlorate reductase subunit A for Nostoc under RFC 10 and RFC 1000 constraints was still running after 15 hours under the original random-substitution behaviour, and completed in seconds afterwards, yielding registry part BBa_268WDVEB. Architecturally the optimiser sits behind POST /optimise so it is usable headlessly without the Svelte interface, and the Docker image removes the platform problem that ViennaRNA and Infernal binaries create on Windows ('docker build -t retisigner . && docker run -p 5050:5050 retisigner'). It ships a user guide, a development guide documenting the system and its API calls, a video tutorial (https://video.igem.org/w/rN6WnodTKYxiNitzfSUVZ3) that optimises BBa_E0040 GFP for Clostridium acetobutylicum ATCC 824, and a public repository at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uni-padua-it/retisigner-svelte (team repo: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/uni-padua-it). It is self-hosted via Docker rather than offered as a hosted public web service, which is the one gap: there is no URL a stranger can click. They also positioned ReTIsigner honestly against the field, reproducing Table 1 of Demissie et al. (2025) and adding their own row with a tilde rather than a tick in the CAI column, and comparing feature-by-feature against the two previous iGEM codon optimisers (HebrewU 2018, MIT 2023). They published an open call for collaboration on 9 August 2026 offering to optimise any team's sequence for their chassis and received no replies, and they say so on the wiki.","stack":["SvelteKit","Svelte","Node.js 22","Python 3.6.9","Flask","Docker (multi-stage)","Miniconda/Conda","ViennaRNA 2.5.1 (RNAplfold)","Infernal 1.1.5","REST/HTTP API","Apache 2.0","iGEM GitLab","Arduino (hardware)"],"techniques":["Simulated annealing search","frequency-weighted synonymous codon sampling","mRNA translation-initiation opening-energy minimisation","Codon Adaptation Index (CAIcal/Sharp-Li definition)","GC-bound feasibility filtering","active restriction-site excision","Kazusa codon-usage-table parsing","permutation testing stratified by strain","in silico benchmarking across GC-spanning hosts","acoustic (piezoelectric) frequency-sweep sensing"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A public survey on space research and biotechnology directly reshaped the team's communication: 76.5% of respondents rated using living organisms for oxygen and water highly while only 28.2% rated colonisation highly, so the team separated the usefulness of the technology from support for settling Mars; 42.2% knew little or nothing about GMOs, so explanations of what they modify and why were rewritten more accessibly; and 94.8% called a genetic safety system fundamental or very important, so the kill switch and confined bioreactor were given more space. Education activities grew out of the same consultations.","ihp":"Human practices is written as a sequence of questions with an explicit 'what we changed' and, unusually, a 'what we couldn't fix' for each. Consultations with photosynthesis and cyanobacteria professors (Nicoletta La Rocca, Daniela Billi) cut the cold-tolerance module. iGEM Darmstadt 2025, who had built the same perchlorate constructs in B. subtilis, warned them off pcrABCD's oxygen sensitivity, which led to testing native NarB first and then compartmentalising pcrABD in heterocysts. A presentation to NASA's Open Science Data Repository Analysis Working Group moved the radiation module from survival-at-high-dose to genome integrity over generations, and pushed them toward a terrestrial use case (perchlorate in groundwater near propellant plants) when the Moon turned out to have no perchlorate. ESA flight-operations engineers (Davide Vertuani, Paolo Ferri) made them reason about the journey, not just arrival. Philosopher Telmo Pievani and space-habitat designer Serena Crotti changed how the bioreactor is framed within a crew's daily life. Most relevant to a dry-lab centre: they took their hardware manual and code to an instrumentation professor and an electronics PhD student (Simone Del Favero, Samuele Faccin) before publishing, and as a result weakened their own claim (the sensor detects gas bubbles, it does not measure oxygen), redrew the circuit with no crossing wires, documented timing resolution, and added a table separating proven from untested.","realWorld":"The team identified a terrestrial application independent of any Mars mission: perchlorate contaminates groundwater near propellant and pyrotechnics manufacturing and is regulated, and biological removal is already an active research area. They were unable to secure an ionising radiation source (INFN Legnaro's Van de Graaff accelerator unavailable; IOV Padova's linear accelerator caps at roughly 6 Gy/min against a 6 kGy LD50), and report that the pprA radiation claim is therefore untested rather than glossing over it.","partners":["iGEM Darmstadt 2025","NASA Open Science Data Repository Analysis Working Group","European Space Agency (flight operations)","University of Padua (Biology, Chemistry, Information Engineering)","University of Rome Tor Vergata","TISIGNER authors (Bhandari, Lim, Gardner)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/new-frontend.avif","caption":"The ReTIsigner interface the team built from scratch in SvelteKit, replacing the original TIsigner form.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/guided-tour.avif","caption":"The in-app guided tour that walks a first-time user through configuring a whole optimisation run.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/results-opt.avif","caption":"The results card: input sequence against an optimised output, with CAI and GC content computed in-app.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/request-schema.avif","caption":"System architecture: the SvelteKit frontend posts a run to Flask, which hands it to a worker pool that shells out to RNAplfold and Infernal.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/optimisation-schema.avif","caption":"One iteration of the simulated annealing search, with the team's two additions: restriction-site removal before scoring and a GC-bound feasibility check.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Fork and extend an existing, cited scientific tool instead of building a new one, and validate the extension at the bench: ReTIsigner's credibility comes from a 2.16-fold wet-lab expression result, a 52%-to-2% failure-rate benchmark, and a published 'what we are entitled to claim' section that names its own limits. A research centre should also copy their packaging discipline, one Docker command plus a documented HTTP endpoint so the engine is reusable headlessly, and their habit of sending documentation to an outside instrumentation expert before publishing it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/judging","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uni-padua-it/retisigner-svelte","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/uni-padua-it"],"rank":59},{"team":"Stanford University","id":"6278","section":"Undergrad","country":"USA","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/","projectTitle":"KelePatch","tagline":"A logic-gated, cell-state-specific RNA therapy for keloid scars, delivered by a microneedle patch shaped to the wound.","summary":"KelePatch builds an ADAR-based RADAR RNA sensor that only switches on inside keloid myofibroblasts, so a therapeutic payload is produced in diseased cells and not in healthy skin. The team designed a 26-part modular sensor collection across five fibrosis transcripts (ADAM12, POSTN, ASPN, COL11A1, SPP1), validated them in HEK293 cells, combined the two best into a dual-input AND gate for extra selectivity, and tested in healthy dermal fibroblasts and keloid-derived fibroblasts; they also paired a sensor with Cas12a2 to show the output is not limited to fluorescent reporters. Alongside the wet lab they built RADAR-Scout, a published web platform that takes a cell type and a gene and returns a ranked, structure-aware list of RADAR sensor candidates plus a mechanistic simulation of how it will behave. It won the Grand Prize because every layer connects: 960,518 single cells of transcriptomics pick the trigger, ViennaRNA folding energy ranks the sensor, ODE models explain the delay they saw at the bench, and a U-Net++ computer vision pipeline turns a photo of a scar into a 3D-printable patch.","awards":["Grand Prize Winner (Undergrad)","Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Therapeutics Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Four stacked layers. (1) Single-cell transcriptomics at scale: a processing pipeline integrating 26 scRNA-seq cohorts across cardiac, cutaneous, renal, hepatic and pulmonary fibrosis - 960,518 cells and 1,728 donor-population observations from CELLxGENE, CELLxGENE Census and GEO - with donor-level statistics averaged at equal donor weight so large-cell-count donors do not dominate. Rather than ordinary differential expression, they score each candidate across three populations (Pathological, Healthy, Bystander) to ask whether a transcript is abundant in the target cell and absent from neighbours, which is what an intracellular sensor actually requires. (2) RNA structure prediction: 19 predictors computed with ViennaRNA 2.7.2 (RNAfold for MFE and ensembles, RNApfold for per-nucleotide unpaired probability, RNAcofold for the sensor-trigger duplex) spanning sequence composition, secondary structure, accessibility and hybridization. (3) Mechanistic kinetics: five interconnected deterministic mass-action ODE models with a stochastic counterpart, covering plasmid nuclear entry, single-trigger plasmid, single-trigger RNA, AND-gate plasmid and AND-gate RNA delivery, built on Ligon et al.'s lipoplex delivery cascade with occupancy resolved into nine pit states and ten endosome states; 82 parameters are tabulated with provenance, and four are fitted by simulated annealing in Python against Cohen et al.'s qPCR nuclear copy-number data (p_entry = 0.107, K_sat = 232, k_TX = 0.273). A nine-state hybridization/editing scheme tracks the joint status of both AND-gate sites. (4) Device physics: finite element analysis using Fick's law, the Stokes-Einstein relation and Darcy's law for LNP diffusion through keloid extracellular matrix, plus a nociceptor-count model for pain.","dataviz":"RADAR-Scout is the visualization: a deployed web app (https://radar-scout.vercel.app/, with a companion dashboard at https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/) where the keloid view ranks 600 filtered genes across five cohorts on the left and plots every candidate sensor for the selected gene on the right, so a target and a sensor are chosen in the same screen, with filters for region type, codon variant, duplex length and isoform, and shortlist/CSV export. Changing the selected tissue reruns the isoform analysis live. The wiki itself is a hand-built site with per-page accent colours and mascot illustrations, and the modelling page is written so a reader can follow each decision: predictor correlations are shown three ways (full Spearman matrix at n = 24, per-replicate rho, then all predictors ranked) and the team states plainly that non-significant predictors are 'unresolved at n = 24 rather than confirmed null'. Parameter tables flag which values are fitted, which are placeholders, and which disagreements remain unresolved.","datascience":"The core result is a replacement for a field convention. The informal RADAR design guideline was to pick the candidate with the lowest GC content. Stanford built a 5x5 experimental library of 25 sensors, measured them by flow cytometry in HEK293 across two replicates (r = 0.94), gated the top 5% mCherry-positive cells and took geometric-mean GFP / geometric-mean mCherry as the output metric, then correlated 19 computational predictors against it. Sensor partition-function folding free energy reached Spearman rho = 0.62-0.63, against -0.29 for GC content and -0.31 for longest homopolymer - so folding energy, not composition, became the ranking filter inside RADAR-Scout. They were honest about what failed: linear, Ridge and Elastic Net regression on the same features reached only Q2 = 0.20, which is explicitly what pushed them from statistical fitting to mechanistic ODEs. Cross-organ analysis of the top 300 genes per organ found 857 of 1,136 distinct genes appeared in only one organ and pairwise Jaccard similarity of 0.050-0.145, so they rejected the idea of a universal fibrosis biomarker set. Isoform filtering uses GTEx Oxford Nanopore long-read data (~150,000 transcripts, 92 samples, 15 tissues) to check the trigger sequence is actually present in the isoforms that cell type expresses.","software":"RADAR-Scout, live at https://radar-scout.vercel.app/ with a dashboard at https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/. It solves a real engineering problem: the Gao lab's published RADAR candidate table has roughly 7 million rows and could not be held in memory, so build_database.py ingests human_RADAR_sensor_candidates.csv.gz into a SQLite database in 100,000-row chunks with an index on the gene column, and the app queries it read-only with 50 candidates per API page. The computer vision pipeline for personalized microneedle patches uses a U-Net++ with a ResNet34 encoder (from segmentation_models.pytorch) to segment keloid wound photographs, then drives the Autodesk Fusion 360 API to emit a CAD/STL file for 3D printing. Separately, SynBase (https://synbase.web.app/home.html) is a released, free, account-based online learning platform. Parts: 26 modular RADAR parts including 15 functional sensors across five transcripts, novel composite synthetic triggers that each test an entire sensor panel for one gene, a dual-input AND gate, and T7 in vitro transcribed capped polyadenylated mRNA versions.","stack":["ViennaRNA 2.7.2 (RNAfold, RNApfold, RNAcofold)","CELLxGENE / CELLxGENE Census","GEO (GSE163973, GSE181316)","Human Dermal Fibroblast Atlas","GTEx long-read (Oxford Nanopore) RNA-seq","Ensembl / GENCODE annotations","Python","SQLite","Vercel","U-Net++ with ResNet34 encoder (segmentation_models.pytorch)","Autodesk Fusion 360 API","Benchling","Firebase (SynBase)"],"techniques":["Deterministic mass-action ODE modelling","Stochastic (occupancy-resolved) simulation","Simulated annealing parameter fitting","Quasi-steady-state approximation for competitive ADAR binding","Nine-state hybridization/editing state machine","Differential expression with pathological/healthy/bystander contrast","Isoform-level expression filtering","RNA minimum free energy and partition-function analysis","Spearman and Pearson predictor screening","Linear/Ridge/Elastic Net regression (reported as insufficient)","Jaccard similarity for cross-organ overlap","Flow cytometry gating (95th percentile mCherry)","Finite element analysis (Fick, Stokes-Einstein, Darcy)","Semantic image segmentation","Parametric CAD generation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Two deliberately linked programmes. SiBRP (Stanford iGEM Bioengineering Research Program) is a free, fully online three-week bioengineering bootcamp for high school students and incoming freshmen, with nine synchronous two-hour sessions plus three supplemental ones, progressing from molecular biology fundamentals through biodesign, genetic circuit design, plasmid design and experimental workflow to entrepreneurship and ethics. Students work in small groups to produce a full research proposal with literature review and experimental design, submitted as a draft, returned with TA feedback, then revised against a published rubric. It drew nearly 5,000 applications from all 50 US states and over 100 countries (up from 321 in its first year), with two-thirds of applicants female and over 60% from low-income and first-generation backgrounds; 230 participated. Nine guest speakers included iGEM co-founder Drew Endy. Because mentored capacity could not meet demand, the team converted the whole curriculum into SynBase (https://synbase.web.app/home.html) - 'Khan Academy, but for synthetic biology' - nine self-paced modules with accounts that save progress, knowledge checks, interactive activities and discussion boards, no application and no deadline, free worldwide. As of 17 October 2026 it had 750+ registered learners and 650+ free-response discussion contributions, with dry lab modules in development, and the team explicitly invites other iGEM teams to use or adapt it for onboarding their own members.","ihp":"The team was trained in Stanford's Biodesign process by Dr Ross Venook, who told them that biodesign means shadowing clinicians rather than searching online, and they built the whole project around that. Ideation ran as a weekly discipline: every member interviewed one person a week - over twenty conversations with professors, researchers and people in their lives - and a team member's experience with ReSurge, an NGO training reconstructive surgeons abroad, surfaced the statistic that 30% of global disease burden is surgical and reframed abnormal scarring as an access problem. Professor Fan Yang told them burns and keloids are separate research areas and doing both would muddle the work, so they picked one. At the Stanford BIOME Shark Tank they pitched to eight judges (faculty, investors, biotech entrepreneurs) who returned written scorecards; the published response table shows each criticism and what they did, including a judge's suggestion to build an AI-CAD platform for microneedles, which became the U-Net++ segmentation-to-Fusion-360 pipeline. When they looked for keloid patient advocacy groups there were almost none, and the one foundation that replied told them to talk to clinicians instead - the team says plainly that physicians cannot stand in for patients' lived experience and documents this as a second-best route. They then contacted 28 Stanford dermatologists and plastic surgeons and interviewed seven, with a standard instrument of Likert-scale ranking statements plus specialty-specific open questions. Three concerns recurred - recurrence within two years, pain and inconvenience of repeated injections, and difficulty getting drug into dense scar tissue - and each changed the design: Dr Teng's point that late-stage keloids are far harder to treat moved the target from prevention to established keloids; Dr Li's recommendation of collagen-scaffold fibroblast and hydrogel mechanotransduction assays changed the validation plan; physicians' complaint about repeated injections, plus Dr Li's endorsement of a patch, drove the wound-shaped microneedle array, held at 900 um because needles over 1000 um are documented to cause bleeding and pain. Dr Miranda consulted her own network on whether viral-derived parts are inflammatory, and the team decided against combining saRADAR with RNA export machinery as a result. With no animal model for keloids (they occur only in humans), they met Genoskin about their ex vivo human skin models and identified that as the next safety step.","realWorld":"The team was explicit at the Shark Tank that a target product profile was secondary to characterising a modular sensor platform other teams and scientists can reuse, and the contribution page is written as a handoff: swap the sensor region for a new transcript, add a second input as an AND gate, deliver as DNA or RNA, attach your own payload. The clinical path is staged and named - validate the Gao lab sensors and their own in HEK293, validate the AND gate, move to keloid and normal human fibroblast lines, add payload, then terminal assay in Genoskin's ex vivo human skin model for immunotoxicity, phenotypic and transcriptomic readouts - and Genoskin representatives engaged and advised on how to run it. The Cas12a2 payload work and the in vitro transcribed mRNA format are both framed as moving toward the form a real therapy would take rather than a reporter demo. SynBase is maintained beyond the competition as a continuing programme rather than a competition artefact.","partners":["ReSurge International","Genoskin","Stanford Biodesign","Stanford Hospital Department of Dermatology","Stanford Department of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery","Stanford BIOME (Biological Interdisciplinary Open Maker Environment)","Bay Area Bioengineering Symposium","Stanford Arc Institute","Gao Lab (RADAR technology)","Covert Lab","CELLxGENE / Chan Zuckerberg Initiative","GTEx Consortium"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig4-trigger-selection.webp","caption":"Trigger selection in RADAR-Scout: 600 filtered genes ranked across five keloid cohorts on the left (POSTN first at DSS 1.288), with every candidate sensor for the selected gene plotted on the right, so target and sensor are chosen in one view.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig10-rho-ranked.webp","caption":"All 19 computational predictors ranked by Spearman rho against sensor output: the two folding-energy terms lead at 0.62-0.63 while GC content, the field's informal selection rule, reaches only -0.29.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m3-cv1-patch.webp","caption":"A microneedle array patch generated from a photograph of a keloid scar: U-Net++ with a ResNet34 encoder segments the wound, and the Autodesk Fusion 360 API turns the mask into a 3D-printable CAD file.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig20-nine-state.webp","caption":"The nine-state hybridization and editing scheme for the AND-gate RADAR sensor; only the doubly-edited state is translated into GFP, and the enumerated transitions are how the system was verified to be complete.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/sibrp-call.webp","caption":"The final session of SiBRP 2026, the team's free three-week online bioengineering bootcamp, with students joining from 24 US states and 40 countries.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Stanford's SiBRP-to-SynBase pivot is the directly copyable move: they ran a small mentored cohort, discovered demand they could not staff (5,000 applications for 230 places), and then converted the exact same curriculum into a free self-paced online course with progress tracking and discussion boards - so the teaching asset outlives the cohort. A high-school-plus centre in Taiwan can do the same with its own course material, and should also adopt their dry-lab habit of testing the field's received wisdom (lowest GC content) against its own measured data and replacing it with a better predictor.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/safety","https://radar-scout.vercel.app/","https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/","https://synbase.web.app/home.html"],"rank":230},{"team":"Princeton","id":"6295","section":"Undergrad","country":"United States","village":"Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/","projectTitle":"SymPleco: Making Precision Sequestration Sym-ple","tagline":"One M13 phage chassis, any cargo — bulk-capacity adsorption with antibody-grade specificity and light-triggered release.","summary":"SymPleco attacks a trade-off that runs through all remediation chemistry: activated carbon captures a lot but cannot tell similar molecules apart, while monoclonal antibodies bind precisely but need harsh pH or chaotropic salts to release and cannot be recovered or scaled. Princeton's answer is a filamentous M13 bacteriophage used as a programmable adsorbent: sortase A-mediated ligation staples engineered fusion proteins onto the N-terminus of every pVIII copy (making 'furry' phage that bypass classical display size limits), with pIII carrying a Cg-Defensin anchor for PLA filter attachment, a VHH7 nanobody for macrophage clearance, and an AsLOV2 optomonobody that holds cargo in the dark and lets go under 450 nm light. Because swapping the target means swapping only the displayed binder, the team paired it with SymPlify, a de novo binder design pipeline, so a new target does not wait on a new antibody. It won on foundational-advance logic: a genuinely modular chassis demonstrated across three deliberately unrelated applications (melanoma exosome sequestration, Parkinson's alpha-synuclein interception, microcystin/BPA water remediation), backed by an unusually self-critical ODE model and an IRB-approved patient engagement programme.","awards":["2nd Runner-up (Undergrad)","Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Measurement — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Foundational Advance Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two distinct computational efforts. SymPlify is a de novo protein binder design pipeline that routes a target by type: a protein chain goes to BindCraft (AlphaFold2-multimer hallucination with gradient descent on logits through softmax, SolubleMPNN sequence design, AF2-monomer re-prediction as a deliberately harsh filter, then PyRosetta physical metrics for binding energy and surface hydrophobicity), while a small-molecule or macrocycle chain goes to RFdiffusion3 all-atom diffusion plus LigandMPNN (eight sequences per backbone) and an independent complex-prediction model. PeSTo supplies hotspot residue scoring, RDKit renders structures and finds pharmacophores, and freesasa computes hydrophobic patch fraction. Separately, the measurement work is backed by a mechanistic ODE model of the recursive Phage Jumpstart Assay (jumpstart.py, jumpstart_mechanistic.py, passage.py, phase2.py, recursion.py) tracking naive, infected and free-particle pools with superinfection exclusion (sigma = 0.05), saturating infection, carrying capacity, and a hybrid-coat infectivity a_h derived from real pIII gene dosage (a_h ~0.729 at m13ko7_copies=20); the 1:11 passage step samples each particle pool by Poisson rather than plain division so a lineage can genuinely go extinct.","dataviz":"SymPlify's web interface is the visualisation centrepiece: an RDKit 3D viewer colours the target by PeSTo binding probability, lists top hotspot residues with confidence scores, lets the user delete low-scoring ones with an x to tighten the patch, and recomputes the difficulty breakdown (spread, hydrophobic surface fraction, globularity) live as the selection changes — plus a warning when residues sit more than 30 A apart, because one binder cannot reach them all. A run-status page shows pipeline stage, target type, elapsed time, auto-resubmits, design count and job ID, and a final ranking screen lets the user click through designs and download .pdb files with or without GS linkers. The model page is unusually transparent as data presentation: it prints the full command reference, labels every parameter as measured, derived or assumed, and ranks assumptions by their sensitivity on the round-2 plate ratio.","datascience":"The Jumpstart assay turned a single colony-ratio readout into a trajectory: because each recursion is both a manipulation and a measurement, repeated rounds on a population being driven toward one genotype reveal behaviour a single ratio cannot. Statistics are handled carefully — intervals bootstrap over wells, leave-one-well-out checks are reported, panels yielding 0-2 Amp colonies out of 165-272 are reported as upper bounds rather than measurements, and a chi-square test (197 on 5 df) establishes that the six Run B constructs are not interchangeable while the team explicitly declines to lean on the ordering because the bottom four are indistinguishable from zero. They also report that three magnitude-based titer estimators disagreed by up to 20,000-fold on the same panel, which is why they built a ratio readout instead. Most notably the model documents its own error history: an initial m13ko7_copies=200 was simply the wrong figure (wild-type M13 rolling-circle copy number rather than M13KO7's actual p15A-driven one), corrected to 10 and then 20 as evidence improved, with the admission that the corrected model is more parameter-sensitive, not less.","software":"SymPlify, an integrated de novo binder design pipeline with a web interface, hotspot prediction, cluster job submission with auto-resubmission, and post-design filtering and ranking; released for the wider synthetic biology community and wet-lab tested on the team's own targets. Team repository: https://github.com/dianalluu/puigem2026, with an AlphaFold deployment for the Princeton Della cluster at https://github.com/09aaronl/alphafold_princeton-della. The ODE model ships as a Python package with 52 automated checks in test_model.py (~80 s full run, ~8 s in fast mode) covering mass-balance and neutrality invariants, the hybrid-coat gene-dosage derivation, the Poisson passage statistics and pinned regression values. Hardware: a 3D-printed PLA gyroid biofilter cartridge whose two winning PITCCH designs are published as downloadable source STEP assemblies.","stack":["BindCraft","AlphaFold2-multimer","AlphaFold2 monomer","RFdiffusion3 (RosettaCommons foundry)","LigandMPNN","SolubleMPNN / ProteinMPNN","PeSTo","PyRosetta","RDKit","freesasa","ChimeraX","Python (NumPy/SciPy ODE integration)","Benchling","SLURM GPU cluster (Princeton Della)"],"techniques":["De novo binder design by AF2 hallucination with gradient descent on logits","All-atom diffusion backbone generation around a fixed ligand","Inverse folding / sequence design","Hotspot (epitope) prediction and patch-tightness scoring","Target difficulty scoring (0-100, Easy to Very Hard) from flexibility, globularity, hotspot patch, size, epitope chemistry","Small-molecule scoring by logP 30%, size 20%, rigidity 20%, TPSA 20%, aromaticity 10%","ODE compartment modelling of phage-host population dynamics","Poisson resampling of passage dilutions to allow true extinction","One-at-a-time parameter sensitivity ranking","Bootstrap confidence intervals over wells and leave-one-well-out robustness","Chi-square test of construct interchangeability","Regression-pinned unit testing of a scientific model"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"PITCCH, the Princeton iGEM Team CAD Competition for Hardware, is the standout: entrants were given a real open engineering problem from the project — design a 3D-printable gyroid biofilter, 20 mm diameter by 25 mm height, maximising contact surface area — along with the team's actual print parameters. It ran free, fully asynchronous and online in a junior division (grades 5-12) and an open division (18+), explicitly permitted responsible AI use, and drew 42 registered submissions from the United States, United Kingdom and Mexico, of which 26 passed dimensional and printability screening before being sliced and computationally ranked by total surface area. Announcement emails went to thousands more via high school superintendents, undergraduate engineering directors and makerspaces, each carrying a project description and iGEM links. The team frames it as citizen science and backed that framing with credit: two winning designs appear on the wiki with the designers named on the attribution form, and the registration terms stated plainly that the team reserves the right to draw inspiration from submissions with attribution. They also postered at symposiums and presented at bioethics exchanges, and at the US-China iGEM Human Practices Summit showed their halted immunology work as a case study in how to pivot.","ihp":"This is the strongest documented chain of design changes of any team here, and several of them cut capability rather than adding it. Dr Bowen Jing's technical advice seeded SymPlify and his steric clash filter was adopted, but the team then cut AlphaFold3 from the pipeline entirely because Dr Martin Pacesa argued it often mispredicts binding sites; Pacesa also supplied PeSTo as the hotspot engine and, after the team ran competing designs to compare, AF2 monomer was implemented on his side of the argument. Dr Reynold Panettieri talked them out of sequestering IL-6 — their original software benchmark target — by pointing out IL-6's dual-sided effects on blood cell production and T-cell regulation, redirecting them toward a niche where cheaper-than-mAb production is the real advantage and toward naming one indication and working from there, which is exactly how the final applications are framed. Dr Douglas Galasko's distinction between biomarkers and toxins moved them off amyloid-beta for Alzheimer's and into the Parkinson's and rabies work. Two multiple myeloma patients, interviewed under Princeton IRB Protocol #20003, independently said the word 'virus' for a bacteriophage was a problem — one noting roughly 80% of myeloma patients are treated in community settings and take their doctor's word — which changed how the team describes the project in all outreach. A former FDA biologics deputy director, Dr Celia Witten, pointed them to CBER's phage therapy work; EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and OPPT staff sent them to the statutes; and marine microbiologist Dr Stephen Giovannoni plus phage ecologist Prof Forest Rohwer turned the Cg-Defensin antimicrobial worry into a specific planned avidity-comparison assay. Dr John Wnek, who conserves Diamondback terrapins, chose their environmental targets: microcystins and BPA, because nearby New Jersey plants discharge effluent half a mile offshore with no process for xenobiotics.","realWorld":"The regulatory path is mapped rather than asserted: phage therapy under FDA CBER for the clinical arms, and statute-level grounding for the environmental arm after meetings with EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and Office of Pollution Prevention and Toxics, with the team careful to state that nothing should be read as EPA guidance. Containment is designed for deployment — the phage stays inside a closed water treatment facility bound to a biodegradable PLA filter so it is retrievable and localised, with macrophage clearance via VHH7 as the biosafety route for therapeutic use. The commercial argument is reconfiguration cost: because capture specificity lives in one genetic part on pVIII, changing target means changing that part and not the media, housing, attachment chemistry or capital cycle, which is the case made against granular activated carbon for PFAS where GAC retains poorly over realistic service lifetimes. Work is mapped to SDG 3.4, 6.3 and 9. Continuation is concrete: further IRB submissions to interview melanoma patients and Parkinson's patients and caregivers, the Rohwer avidity assay, qPCR of M13KO7 copy number to settle the model's most sensitive parameter, and phage hydrogels as an alternative anchor that would remove the Cg-Defensin concern altogether.","partners":["Dr Martin Pacesa (BindCraft author)","Dr Bowen Jing (computational biology)","Dr Reynold Panettieri, Rutgers Translational Medicine and Science","Dr Jared Radbel, Robert Wood Johnson Medical School","Dr Douglas Galasko (neurology)","Dr Celia Witten, former deputy director, FDA biologics center","US EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and Office of Pollution Prevention and Toxics","Dr John Wnek (environmental conservation, Diamondback terrapin)","Dr Stephen Giovannoni (marine microbiology)","Prof Forest Rohwer (marine phage ecology)","Prof Ned Wingreen and Lawrence Abad (phage biology)","Prof Jared Toettcher (faculty PI, IRB Protocol #20003)","Princeton Institutional Biosafety Committee","Multiple myeloma patient advocacy organisations","US-China iGEM Human Practices Summit","IDT, GenScript, ODI (sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/symplify-hotspots-protein.avif","caption":"SymPlify's hotspot selection screen: the protein target rendered in 3D and coloured by PeSTo binding probability, with top hotspot residues listed by confidence so low-scoring ones can be removed to leave the focused patch of three to five that BindCraft works best with.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/symplify-filtered-designs.avif","caption":"SymPlify's ranking and filtering view, where accepted binder designs are sorted by AlphaFold confidence metrics and PyRosetta physical scores and can be downloaded as .pdb files with or without GS linkers.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/hardware-pitcch-surface-area.avif","caption":"Ranked printable surface area for the 26 PITCCH entries that passed dimensional and printability screening, plotted against reference geometries — public CAD submissions evaluated computationally and used to pick the biofilter geometry.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/saf-macrophage-uptake-gfp.avif","caption":"Overlaid phase-contrast and green-fluorescence micrograph of cultured macrophages after incubation with GFP-expressing phage, showing uptake by the VHH7 clearance arm. Scale bar 150 micrometres.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy PITCCH: hand the public a genuine, unsolved sub-problem from your own project with real constraints and real print parameters, judge entries computationally on a single objective metric, then credit the winners by name and actually build their design in. It converts outreach into contributed engineering at almost no cost, and it works for high-school students because spatial creativity, not synthetic biology training, is what the task rewards. Pair it with Princeton's habit of writing down which model parameters are measured, derived or merely assumed — and publishing the history of their own corrected mistakes — which is the cheapest credibility a student dry lab can buy.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/design-rationale","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/application","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/safety-and-security","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/judging"],"rank":205},{"team":"Baltimore","id":"6329","section":"High School","country":"United States","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/","projectTitle":"KeraTINT","tagline":"A chromoprotein-keratin-binding-peptide fusion that colours hair and repairs it in the same step","summary":"Baltimore Biocrew, based at the Baltimore Underground Science Space community lab, engineered fusion proteins joining chromoproteins (aeblue, UM) to keratin-binding peptides so that a semi-permanent dye deposits colour while reinforcing the hair shaft. The entrepreneurship work was run through iGEM's Venture Creation Labs curriculum, the same incubator route their 2023 project RhizeUp took, and it produced an OKR table with numeric key results, a scored risk register, a US-specific patent strategy and a competitor map. Market evidence rests on two IRB-approved consumer surveys (56 then 54 responses, translated into Chinese and Filipino) plus named interviews with a cosmetics founder, two L'Oreal North America hair-colour-lab staff, a cosmetology student and the Boeke Lab at NYU Langone. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship in the High School section.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"iGEM Venture Creation Labs curriculum as the scaffold, Golden Circle, Unique Value Proposition, Lean Business Model Canvas, SWOT, a seven-phase Objectives and Key Results table with business-day estimates and fixed-cost lines, a risk register with a published 1-5 impact x 1-5 likelihood scoring rubric and named owners, direct and indirect and future competitor tiers, and the iGEM judging ballot answered question by question.","dataviz":"Interactive click-through SWOT, a nine-slide pitch deck embedded as a carousel, a lean canvas, a long-term impact-assessment graphic, a 2027-2029 timeline, and a horizontally scrollable OKR and risk table. Survey results are reported as percentages and cross-tabulated by language group and age.","datascience":"Two-round survey analysis with a genuine iteration: a bimodal distribution on grey-coverage priority in Survey 1 (a quarter rating it 1, nearly half rating it 10) was hypothesised to be two age cohorts, and Survey 2 confirmed it by adding age, hair type and prior chemical-treatment questions. Reported figures include 64.3 percent rating less hair damage a 10/10 priority, 89 percent rating it 8 or above, 50 percent using at-home boxed kits and only 25.9 percent relying solely on salons.","software":"","stack":["Google Forms","chromoprotein-keratin-binding peptide fusion constructs"],"techniques":["fusion protein design","colour retention and wash-cycle testing (planned)","cytotoxicity assay development (started, not completed)","pH and thermal stability assessment (literature-based)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Surveys were deliberately multilingual, translated into Chinese and Filipino to reach their own communities, and the results split cleanly by language and age in ways that changed the product thesis: younger English-speaking respondents wanted fashion blues, pinks and purples, which happens to be what their chromoproteins produce, while older Chinese and Filipino respondents wanted grey coverage and natural shades. They also found that the phrase 'genetically engineered' on a label worried more respondents than it attracted, with several linking it to harmful chemicals or illness, and responded by planning a consumer FAQ booklet distributed through partner salons instead of a call centre. They presented at the July 2026 Mid-Atlantic Meetup alongside eight other regional teams.","ihp":"A Closed-Loop Framework structured each stakeholder conversation into relevance, insight, integration and future question. IRB approval from the BUGSS Board of Directors was obtained before surveying, which is unusual rigour at high-school level. Specific conversations changed specific decisions: Savannah Shumaker (chemist, co-founder and CEO of Eight Count Cosmetics) pushed branding toward audience values and simplified their science communication, and suggested contacting cosmetology schools, which led to Rachel Lu; Lindsay Menzer and Yun Yong Jang (L'Oreal Hair North America, Head of Hair Color Lab) flagged patenting during early testing, which launched the US patent-strategy investigation; the Boeke Lab at NYU Langone recommended storage conditions for chromoproteins, prompting a preservation study; and a question from another team at the Mid-Atlantic Meetup about pH and temperature sensitivity, which they had deprioritised, is what sent them to the Boeke Lab in the first place.","realWorld":"This is a credible early-stage venture plan with the finance section still hollow. What is genuinely strong is the execution scaffolding. The OKR table is the best artefact: it sets falsifiable targets with costs and durations attached, including catalogue 15 salons statewide, a minimum of 10 qualitative interviews, at least 5 Letters of Intent, 10 investor pitches, 10,000 dollars from stakeholder investment plus research grants, cell viability above 70 percent in cytotoxicity assays, dye longevity of 40 wash cycles, protein stability at 50 degrees C, 1,000 sales per month and two new production labs in other mid-Atlantic states. Named funding targets are real and local (Wexford SciTech Venture Fund, TEDCO grants, Connect Labs at 4MLK as prospective lab space, youth competitions such as Blue Ocean, Paradigm and Slingshot). Crucially, every one of these is still an aspiration: the Letters of Intent are a key result, not a signed document; the grant is applied for, not won; there is no registered entity, though forming an LLC is floated inside the liability risk row. The regulatory path is the most concretely researched part of the whole analysis and is credible: they identify that the FDA Office of Cosmetics and Colors certifies coal-tar and synthetic colour additives but that a chromoprotein appears to fall into neither bucket, name 21 CFR parts 73 and 74 subpart C, name the Color Certifications Inquiry Branch as the body to ask, and note that certified and uncertified colours carry legally distinct names. The IP strategy is equally specific and actually actionable: a provisional patent on the aeblue-keratin fusion first, converted to a non-provisional within twelve months, with USPTO micro-entity status claimed for an 80 percent fee reduction, and they check their own eligibility against the 262,380 dollar gross-income threshold. Market sizing, by contrast, is the weakest link, resting on Grand View Research hair-colour market reports rather than any bottom-up estimate, with no price point, no cost of goods and no unit economics anywhere. Continuation is plausible rather than secured: they state plainly that as a high-school iGEM team they can never test on human hair, so KeraTINT must pursue third-party testing or operate independently of iGEM after the competition, and they sequence cytotoxicity, pH and thermal assays as the first post-Jamboree work. The precedent helps their case, since the same team's 2023 RhizeUp project went through the same incubator.","partners":["iGEM Venture Creation Labs / Venture Foundry (incubator curriculum)","Baltimore Underground Science Space (BUGSS) community lab and its Board of Directors (IRB approval)","Savannah Shumaker, chemist and co-founder/CEO, Eight Count Cosmetics","Lindsay Menzer and Yun Yong Jang, L'Oreal Hair North America, Head of Hair Color Lab","Rachel Lu, cosmetology student and freelance makeup artist","Dr. Jef Boeke and Aleksandra Wudzinska, Boeke Lab, NYU Langone Health","Mid-Atlantic Meetup regional iGEM teams"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/lbmc.avif","caption":"KeraTINT lean business model canvas identifying local salons and cosmetic companies as first customers","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/timeline.avif","caption":"Commercialisation timeline from 2027 stakeholder re-engagement to a 2029 salon product roll-out","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/pitch-deck/2.avif","caption":"Slide from the nine-slide KeraTINT pitch deck covering market sizing and distribution channels","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/impact-assessment.avif","caption":"Long-term positive and negative impact assessment for consumers and the wider hair-care market","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Steal the OKR table: every commercialisation objective gets a numeric key result, a business-day estimate and a fixed-cost line, including scientific gates such as cell viability above 70 percent and dye longevity of 40 wash cycles, so a business plan becomes a checklist a successor team can inherit. Also copy getting institutional IRB approval before running consumer surveys, which both protects participants and makes the resulting data citable.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/","https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/human-practices/"],"rank":7},{"team":"NJU-China","id":"6337","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/","projectTitle":"Starase Atlas — Atlas-BRIDGE, Atlas-COMPASS and Atlas-EDGE for enzyme discovery","tagline":"Given a target reaction and limited experimental resources, which enzyme candidates should a researcher test first?","summary":"NJU-China built a three-part platform around one decision: which enzyme to test next. Atlas-BRIDGE (Broad Retrieval with Inference-Driven Gated Experts) is a dual-tower contrastive retrieval model that will rank any protein it can encode, then lets specialist 'expert' models and two kinds of memory nudge that ranking only where their evidence genuinely applies; Atlas-COMPASS is a natural-language agent front end that resolves entities before it computes anything; Atlas-EDGE is a MySQL/graph knowledge base of compound nodes and enzyme edges built from UniProt, Rhea and ChEBI. The model's recommendation was then taken to the bench: M5AW86 was predicted and confirmed to convert FPP to beta-farnesene by GC-MS, TLC and 1H NMR against empty-vector controls. It won Best Software & AI Project and was nominated for Best Model.","awards":["Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Software & AI Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Atlas-BRIDGE is a machine-learning information-retrieval model, not a kinetic one, and its central design idea is worth copying regardless of domain: separate *eligibility to be ranked* from *availability of evidence*. The base model ('Broad') is a two-tower encoder — one tower for protein sequence, one for reaction transformation — trained with a multi-positive contrastive objective that respects the many-to-many nature of enzyme data, L_MP = lambda*L_R2E + (1-lambda)*L_E2R, where each directional loss sums over the full set of known positives rather than treating a single pair as the truth. Because an unrecorded enzyme-reaction pair is not a true negative, they apply positive-unlabeled (PU) learning plus sequence-cluster masking to decide which pairs are even admitted for contrast, and because experimental screening only tests the top of a list they add an explicit head-ranking hinge, L_head = softplus(n_K - p* + m), pushing the best positive above the K-th admissible negative, with hard negatives mined from the candidates the current model confuses most. The final score is additive and auditable: S = S_Broad + alpha_LT*Delta_LT + alpha_epi*Delta_epi + sum_k g_k*Delta_k. The Gated Experts are plug-in models carrying functional, structural/mechanistic or family/domain evidence; each must publish candidate-wise scores *and an availability mask*, has its applicability gate, normalisation and maximum correction magnitude locked on separate validation data before any external test, and reverts exactly to the prior ranking wherever its evidence is missing — so absent data is never silently read as a low score. Long-term memory replays the training-cutoff catalytic graph at inference, projecting 16 annotated protein neighbours and 8 annotated reaction neighbours back into the Broad space with query-conditioned permission weights fitted on 5,216 annotated development associations, and it only re-sorts the top 1,000 Broad candidates. Episodic memory handles positives that appear *after* the training cutoff (new curation, new assays, a collaborator's private hit) without retraining anything: each support is encoded in the frozen Broad space and combined by parameter-free attention, a_i = softmax(zscore(q^T s_i)), m_epi = sum a_i s_i, with authority scaled by support count, internal coherence and conflict with the baseline; positives already in the training graph are stripped out first to prevent double-counting with long-term memory. Atlas-COMPASS wraps this in a DeepSeek-API-driven agent that first resolves every reaction, protein and compound in a plain-language question to a Rhea / UniProt / ChEBI record and shows a confirmation card the user must approve before anything is computed, then plans the retrieval, and can additionally run thermodynamic Max-min Driving Force analysis and E. coli iML1515 genome-scale flux checks on candidate pathways when asked.","dataviz":"The visual argument is carried by the evaluation figures rather than by decoration. Benchmark comparisons are plotted as grouped MRR / Hit@10 / Hit@20 bars against EnzymeCAGE on two external test sets, with the harder Orphan-335 set shown alongside Enzyme-405 so the reader sees where the gain is largest. The four-configuration study is drawn as a controlled 2x2 (reference, coverage-only, ranker-only, complete system) in both retrieval directions, so the figure itself separates 'we found more candidates' from 'we ranked them better'. Ablation panels remove one evidence block at a time in both directions. Seed-count sensitivity is shown both as performance-versus-number-of-positive-seeds line plots under two evaluation protocols (random-positive and the much stricter protein-cluster-cold) and as grouped Hit@3/10/20 bars for one, two and three seeds. The model page also renders the architecture itself — dual-tower geometry, anchored reranking that visibly protects the base order, and query-level gating of each expert — as labelled schematics, and the five development cycles are laid out as a descending staircase so the reader walks the DBTL history. Atlas-EDGE's own interface is the other half of the dataviz: a hand-built SVG graph in which compounds are nodes and enzymes are directional edges, with composite edges that collapse multiple enzymes between two compounds and expand on click, click-through compound and enzyme info cards, zoom, drag, filtering, path highlighting and URL-synced view state.","datascience":"Evaluation is the strongest part of this project and is unusually self-sceptical. External benchmarking used the EnzymeCAGE authors' own test sets under their protocol: 21,505 training-unseen positive relationships ranked over the full encodable universe of 185,918 protein candidates (11,081 reaction candidates in the reverse direction). Reported gains: Enzyme-405 MRR +16.0% and Hit@10 +4.55pp; Orphan-335 MRR +44.0%, Hit@10 +17.32pp, Hit@20 +18.51pp. Four ablation studies follow, including a difficulty-balanced re-aggregation that freezes every prediction, candidate set and rank and changes *only* the averaging — giving equal weight to seen, protein-cold, reaction-cold and double-cold exposure classes and then to the graph-degree strata within each — explicitly to stop frequent, highly connected, easy relations dominating a micro-average. Domain experts were tested only on queries where they are applicable, with deterministic family held-out splits for P450 and phosphatase and a frozen semantic applicability gate selecting 18 of 1,042 strict double-cold queries for terpene synthases; gains were P450 MRR 0.0394 to 0.0503, phosphatase 0.3012 to 0.3077, TPS 0.1225 to 0.3114 with Hit@10 11.11% to 33.33%, candidate sets and core scores held fixed. Wet-lab closure: M5AW86 was recommended for FPP to beta-farnesene and confirmed by a converging evidence chain (differential GC-MS peak at ~5.5 min with base peak m/z 69, fragments at 41/81/93/107/121/135 and a low-abundance molecular ion at m/z 204; TLC spots absent from empty-vector controls; 1H NMR olefinic signals at delta 6.3-6.4 and 5.0-5.3 ppm matched to a literature reference). They also publish a disconfirmation: for D9XD61 the model predicted (+)-T-muurolol but the isolated product was identified as 7-epi-alpha-eudesmol, and they say so. Preliminary GC-MS hit rate was 7 of 14 recommended candidates (50%). The interface discipline matches: the 0-100 displayed model score is explicitly documented as 'not a catalytic success rate', not activity or yield, not comparable across retrieval routes, and a high rank means 'deserves priority verification, not that it has been biologically proven'; 'unknown' in a compatibility cell means missing records, not incompatibility.","software":"Both instruments are Apache 2.0 open source in the official iGEM GitLab repository, deployable with git clone plus bash scripts/bootstrap.sh, setup_release.sh --download and --check, then manage.sh configure-key / start / status. Linux with Python 3.12 is the validated environment, ~5 GiB for model assets, WSL2 for Windows, macOS explicitly flagged as not validated — and crucially no GPU is required, since validation and inference run on CPU. The DeepSeek API key is stored in a git-ignored runtime directory and the docs tell deployers to put the service behind HTTPS with access control, rate limiting, log monitoring and API quotas, and to explain how external model services handle data. Atlas-EDGE is a full production stack: React + TypeScript + Vite front end (hand-drawn SVG graph, History API URL sync, localStorage session persistence, AbortController to cancel stale searches), FastAPI + Uvicorn + Pydantic + async SQLAlchemy/aiomysql backend on a /api/v1 prefix, MySQL 8.0.46, a four-round UniProt REST crawl plus Rhea SPARQL and ChEBI flat files, pandas/RDKit ETL into entity and association tables with a unified sha1-keyed search index, local NCBI BLAST+ (blastp/makeblastdb, run in a thread with a mutex guarding database construction and an sha1 cache key), Ketcher for draw-a-molecule structure search, and DeepSolNet run locally with original weights to generate per-protein solubility scores used as a search filter.","stack":["Python 3.11 / 3.12","PyTorch (dual-tower encoders)","FastAPI","Uvicorn","Pydantic","SQLAlchemy asyncio + aiomysql","MySQL 8.0.46","React + TypeScript + Vite","pandas","RDKit","NCBI BLAST+","Ketcher","DeepSolNet","DeepSeek API","UniProt REST","Rhea SPARQL","ChEBI flat files","Pfam","cobrapy / iML1515 genome-scale model","scikit-learn / random forest / gradient boosting (experts)"],"techniques":["dual-tower contrastive retrieval","multi-positive InfoNCE-style loss","positive-unlabeled learning","sequence-cluster negative masking","hard-negative mining","listwise head-ranking hinge loss","mixture-of-experts gating with availability masks","retrieval-augmented long-term graph memory","parameter-free attention episodic memory (few-shot, training-free update)","protein language model embeddings","differential reaction fingerprints (DRFP)","learning-to-rank (LambdaMART lineage)","ablation studies","cold-start / double-cold stratified evaluation","difficulty-balanced metric re-aggregation","MRR and Hit@K ranking metrics","graph database modelling","pathway search over a directed multigraph","Max-min Driving Force thermodynamic analysis","genome-scale flux balance analysis (iML1515)","natural-language agent with entity grounding"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Nine primary-school lessons from March to July 2026 (five plant lessons, four summer science lessons), each treated as its own Design-Build-Test-Learn unit: Design fixed audience and objectives, Build made slides and materials, Test watched what pupils actually explained and where they needed help, Learn turned the observed problems into changes to the next lesson. From that they distilled SPARK as a reusable design and review lens — See, Probe, Ask, Record and Respond, Keep improving — and then applied it back onto their secondary-school intro and lab teaching and their public science conversations at Starlight Market. The handover is concrete: slides, activity materials, a lesson design checklist, guidelines for experimental and simulation activities, a feedback and evaluation form, and 'SPARK-Edu Skill', an AI-assisted guide that encodes their principles, workflow and examples so a future education team can adapt rather than restart.","ihp":"Human Practices is presented as evidence that changed the build, with a counted audit trail: 24 evidence records, 6 stakeholder lenses, 4 design shifts, 3 intended influence pathways, across academic (8), iGEM and user (4), industry (5), technology transfer (3), safety (2) and education contacts. The four shifts are stated as before/after pairs: from a black-box compatibility score to explicit experimental prioritisation; from searching known pairs to open exploration with uncertainty; from ranked names only to explainable recommendations; from a post-hoc warning to responsibility by design. The origin is traceable: in November 2025 Jing Shi pointed out that a promising enzyme record may behave differently in a new host, Fei Zhou described the preparatory work caused by scattered information, and Long Wang warned that sequence similarity is not function — which is why the platform separates recorded associations (the factual layer) from model candidates (the prediction layer) in every view. The team also took on the dual-use question directly: if an AI tool makes finding functional enzymes easier, which questions should it decline to answer lightly, how should high-risk queries be handled, and what may private data be used for.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship case is for a screening *service* rather than another database: computational screening carried through to wet-lab validation so the customer receives a confirmed candidate, with the pipeline and its documentation left behind as a reusable toolkit and the methods and data kept public. Numbers are stated with their baselines: enzyme-to-reaction accuracy 15.32% (up from 8.47% in the team's own historical runs), a 1.92x top-20 hit rate against CLIPZyme (15.97% vs 8.33%) over 166,202 benchmark candidates, a 50% preliminary GC-MS hit rate (7 of 14), an estimated core enterprise TAM of RMB 14.42M annually in the baseline forecast, and a proposed tiered price range of RMB 4.3k to 319.5k per year from Academic to Enterprise Advanced. They are candid that the current demo only accepts target reactions and candidate proteins and returns rankings, with traceability, version management and full decision support still to come. The stated audiences are research labs (inspectable enzyme discovery), the iGEM community (reflective HP practice) and the bio-manufacturing ecosystem (governed, compatible pilots).","partners":["Nanjing University School of Life Sciences","Nanjing University School of Intelligence Science and Technology","Nanjing University School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering","Nanjing University School of Software","Vazyme","WuXi","BioSphere"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/model.avif","caption":"Atlas-BRIDGE architecture: a shared dual-tower retrieval geometry that will score any encodable protein or reaction, with query-level gated experts and dual (long-term graph plus episodic) memory applying additive corrections only inside their valid scope.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/7.avif","caption":"The Broad Retrieval dual-tower architecture for bidirectional enzyme-reaction ranking: one tower encodes protein sequence, the other the reaction transformation, trained with a multi-positive contrastive loss plus a head-ranking constraint so the top of the list — the part experiments actually test — is sharpened.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/seed-performance-lines-v2.avif","caption":"Performance as a function of the number of positive seeds, plotted under two evaluation protocols — random-positive and the much stricter protein-cluster-cold — so the reader can see how much of the apparent gain survives when close homologs of the known positives are withheld.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/9.avif","caption":"Query-level gating of the functional, structural/mechanistic, family/domain and memory experts. Each expert must emit an availability mask as well as scores, and reverts exactly to the prior ranking wherever its evidence is missing, so absent data is never read as a low score.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Make every model output carry an availability mask, so 'we have no evidence about this candidate' and 'this candidate scores badly' are never the same number — and separate the factual layer (what a database records) from the prediction layer (what your model guesses) in every table and figure you publish. Then re-aggregate your headline metric with equal weight across difficulty strata to check your result is not just the easy cases.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/compass/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/edge/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/proof/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/entrepreneurship/"],"rank":33},{"team":"Oxford","id":"6391","section":"Undergrad","country":"United Kingdom","village":"Agriculture","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/","projectTitle":"NECTAR","tagline":"An end-to-end platform for designing, validating, producing and deploying RNAi interventions, stress-tested on Varroa destructor","summary":"NECTAR is a programmable RNAi pipeline: FORAGER (computational dsRNA design with off-target screening against the Apis mellifera transcriptome), microbial production in yeast, and delivery to hives in a pollen patty. Varroa destructor, the single biggest threat to honeybees, is the first real-world case study. The team built HIVE, an explicit four-step human-practices decision procedure (Hear, Investigate, Verdict, Evaluate) run over six open project questions with 28+ named stakeholders including US EPA/FDA/USDA staff, the Chair of the UK Advisory Committee on Releases to the Environment, and the Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity. Stakeholder evidence changed the chassis (living engineered Snodgrassella alvi to heat-inactivated yeast), the delivery format, the off-target screening stage, the survey instrument and the definition of success.","awards":["Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Sustainable Development Impact — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Agriculture Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"FORAGER, a three-stage standardised dsRNA design pipeline: target gene identification (most abundant transcripts of well-connected genes, weighted by known Varroa RNAi data), within-gene region selection (endogenous Varroa siRNA sequence features, guide competence, RNAplfold target accessibility, combined as three equally weighted complementary evidence layers rather than one fused score), and off-target analysis. Also a BEEHAVE-based colony population model and a bespoke partial-equilibrium economic model.","dataviz":"Hand-drawn mechanism illustrations throughout (FORAGER stages, the five-step path of a dose through the hive, the SDG goal map), a geocoded stakeholder map with latitude/longitude per interviewee, and per-cycle engineering tables; the wiki is a custom React/Astro-style build with light and dark themes.","datascience":"Construction of a clean Varroa destructor small-RNA dataset from public sequencing, basic population feature analysis of that sRNA pool, sequence feature analysis, whole-region homology and local exact-match screening against the honeybee transcriptome (16-nt strict mapping plus relaxed whole-region similarity), BLAST-based non-target screening, and a documented limitations table stating that the three evidence layers are equally weighted only because no Varroa RNAi efficacy data existed to fit weights.","software":"FORAGER is released with one file per stage and a documented frontmatter/body contract so other teams can re-run it; the HIVE method and its full interview question bank are released under CC BY 4.0. Hive insert and PCR-tube feeding adaptor are downloadable 3D-printable hardware.","stack":["ViennaRNA / RNAplfold","BLAST","Bowtie","BEEHAVE (NetLogo)","Boltz structure prediction","molecular docking","R","Python","React / Astro wiki build"],"techniques":["functional network connectivity analysis (STRING-type)","mRNA abundance weighting","small-RNA population profiling","RNA secondary structure and accessibility prediction","MS2 hairpin array folding optimisation (6% to 67% complete-array folding)","partial-equilibrium economic modelling with supply/demand elasticities","sensitivity analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Buzz In, a three-episode stakeholder podcast built deliberately to put stakeholders who disagreed with each other into contact rather than collecting views in isolation: episode 1 paired Varroa-resistance academic Prof. Stephen Martin with commercial beekeeper Paul Horton on whether to treat at all; episode 2 brought together the CEO of the Australian Honey Bee Industry Council, Amateur Beekeepers Australia and Hive & Wellness; episode 3 set the project against social, environmental and economic lenses with a social scientist, a bee researcher and an agricultural economist. GMO 101, a joint Zoom workshop with NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM on 8 October 2026, measured participation through Nearpod (24 participants, 83% took part). A beekeeper survey received a favourable opinion from a subcommittee of the Oxford Central University Research Ethics Committee (ref MS IDREC 3973787), with public task as the lawful basis and informed consent before questions; 100+ respondents, 63% reported struggling with Varroa at mean severity 5.74/10 and 70% said they would be open to a GMO-based treatment. School sessions at St Clare's summer course were run as a two-way choice exercise (present two delivery routes with honest trade-offs, ask which students would accept, measure the acceptance shift before and after) rather than a lecture. Plus street interviews, a bee campaign, Instagram and TikTok.","ihp":"Oxford's IHP is the strongest kind: a documented decision procedure with a mandatory 'How we implemented the advice to change NECTAR' field on every one of 28+ interview write-ups, and a rule that 'We considered their feedback is not an implementation'. The largest single change is the chassis. Danny Le Feuvre (CEO, Australian Honey Bee Industry Council) and Mike Allerton (VP and Biosecurity Officer, Amateur Beekeepers Australia) both preferred a persistent living engineered Snodgrassella alvi gut symbiont because it minimises beekeeper labour; the team did not simply follow them but took the question to regulators, and Laura Bowden (GM manager, Science and Advice for Scottish Agriculture), Elizabeth Frost (NSW Department of Primary Industries), Tom Hartley (Senior Certification Officer, Soil Association Certification) and the Scottish Government Honey Bee Health Team pushed the opposite way, so the team switched to heat-inactivated, dried engineered yeast and stated plainly what that cost: the persistence that made S. alvi attractive, meaning repeat dosing and more beekeeper labour, against the preference of beekeepers they had interviewed. Austein McLoughlin of the Convention on Biological Diversity Secretariat then confirmed the inactivated product is not a living modified organism under the Cartagena Protocol, but also refused to let the team stop at SDG alignment, pushing them to ask whether NECTAR produces 'measurable improvement' - which is why the sustainability analysis widened to the whole NECTAR lifecycle and built baseline comparisons into the modelling. A joint meeting with Mike Mendelsohn and Alan Reynolds (US EPA), Adam Moyer and Laura Epstein (FDA) and Alan Pearson (USDA) then 'prevented us from treating our shift to heat-inactivated yeast as a universal regulatory shortcut', forcing jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction regulatory mapping instead of one global classification. The deepest economic chain runs through Prof. Wally Thurman (Agricultural and Resource Economics, NC State, interviewed 29 July 2026) and Prof. Brittney Goodrich (Agricultural and Consumer Economics, Illinois, 7 August 2026): Thurman set five methodological commitments (start from an average commercial operation not an industry aggregate; use existing treatments as the counterfactual; assess costs and revenues not survival rates; use simple partial equilibrium; restrict to paid pollination markets), and Goodrich's operation-level cost structure redefined the cost of Varroa to include labour, colony replacement and lost honey and pollination revenue rather than just the price of acaricide. The team then ran the chain backwards - what a beekeeper can afford, to allowable cost per dose, to allowable manufacturing cost, to required mass of intact dsRNA per gram of dry yeast - converting 'high expression' into a pass/fail wet-lab specification of 0.005 mg dsRNA per g yeast for US viability and 0.05 mg/g for both markets, against a fermentation cost of about 2.33 USD per gram. They also deliberately recruited dissent: the Oxfordshire Natural Beekeeping Group (about 20 chemical-free hobbyists) and treatment-free beekeeper Mark Sandham argued that treating removes the selection pressure that would produce tolerant bees, an argument against the entire premise; rather than abandoning the project the team narrowed its target user to commercial operations and wrote that OxNatBees changed 'not the molecular mechanism of NECTAR, but the problem we believed NECTAR should - and should not - try to solve'. Smaller but equally traceable: Lord John Krebs (first FSA Chairman) and Prof. Jim Dunwell (Chair, ACRE) made environmental fate and off-target effects explicit engineering requirements, moving non-target organisms into the FORAGER off-target screening stage and adding in vivo dsRNA-persistence experiments; Prof. Paul Lam (environmental chemistry, Hong Kong Metropolitan University) drove the adoption of BLAST off-target screening and reframed public engagement as risk management rather than promotion; Dr Michael Morrison (HeLEX, Oxford Law) rewrote the survey instrument so it asked separately about pollen patties, constitutively active engineered bacteria and inducible engineered bacteria rather than about 'GMOs' in general; Dr Théotime Colin's point about secondary pests prompted dsRNA constructs for wax moth and small hive beetle; Prof. Giles Budge's reassurance kept the adult-bee oral delivery route alive and produced the 300/500/700 bp length comparison; and Melanie Teece (Head of Technical, Hilltop Honey) and Wade Ford (Hive & Wellness) turned honey purity into a reason for additional in vivo work. The published HIVE guide names its own failure modes (interviewing agreeable people, treating 'the public' as one stakeholder, letting the Verdict drift into a summary, consulting regulators too late to change anything) and requires every Verdict to state what was decided, what was rejected and what that cost.","realWorld":"The need is framed with numbers rather than adjectives: Varroa is an invasive alien species across most of its range and the most serious pest of the western honeybee; Australia's failed eradication attempt cost A$101 million and destroyed about 30,000 hives, and surveyed US commercial operations lost around 62% of colonies in 2024-25, estimated at over $600 million. SDGs are chosen, not collected: 15 (Life on Land) and 2 (Zero Hunger) are the primary goals with 17 and 4 as enabling goals, each mapped to named sub-targets (15.8 on invasive alien species is the headline, plus 15.1, 15.5, 15.9), and the analysis extends into the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets 7, 10, 11 and 17 at the CBD Secretariat's suggestion. The team then writes a dedicated negative-impacts section on goal conflicts - accessibility across economic status, pollution, ecological threat, and whether healthier managed colonies put pressure on wild pollinators (goal 15 against goal 2) - and admits an unresolved contradiction: stakeholders insisted cost was decisive while 48% of their own survey respondents ranked cost least important, with the honest line 'We have not resolved this'. Deployment rides on existing practice (dsRNA-bearing heat-killed yeast in a conventional pollen patty laid over the frames, plus a 3D-printable hive insert), the regulatory path is mapped per jurisdiction after the EPA/FDA/USDA conversation, and the cost model gives both a per-colony net private benefit and the titre threshold manufacturing must hit. They are unusually candid about what is not demonstrated: no dsRNA titre was measured against the model thresholds, the yeast could not be cloned and the E. coli route gave no detectable dsRNA, so 'this is a case for the approach, not a demonstration of the product'. Handover material - FORAGER, the HIVE cycle and question bank, the bee research guide, protocols and hardware files - is released CC BY 4.0 for future teams.","partners":["Amateur Beekeepers Australia","Australian Honey Bee Industry Council","Hive & Wellness Australia","NSW Department of Primary Industries","Science and Advice for Scottish Agriculture (SASA)","Scottish Government Honey Bee Health Team","Advisory Committee on Releases to the Environment (ACRE)","Soil Association Certification","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity","US EPA, FDA and USDA","Hilltop Honey","Sustainable Conservation / Almond Board of California","Oxfordshire Natural Beekeeping Group","NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM","University of Oxford Department of Biology (Prof. Geraldine Wright)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/drawings/sdg-description.avif","caption":"NECTAR at the centre of four chosen SDGs - 15 Life on Land and 2 Zero Hunger as primary goals, 17 Partnerships and 4 Quality Education as enabling goals - each with its own justification rather than a logo wall","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/education/gmo-webinar-zoom.avif","caption":"GMO 101, the joint Zoom workshop with NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM on 8 October 2026, with participation measured through Nearpod","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/bee-research-guide/walking-to-the-hives.avif","caption":"The team walking to the hives - fifteen members with zero beekeeping experience at the start of the project","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/education/street-interview.avif","caption":"Street interviews on public attitudes to GMOs in bee treatment, run as risk management rather than promotion after advice from Prof. Paul Lam","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Make the write-up template itself enforce integration: every stakeholder record must carry a 'what this changed' field, and every verdict must name what was decided, what was rejected and what that cost - then run the affordability chain backwards from what the user can pay to a numerical pass/fail specification for the bench.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/economic-modelling","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/case-studies","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/beekeeper-survey","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/project-description"],"rank":47},{"team":"NFLSHS-CHINA","id":"6439","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/","projectTitle":"CroTeen","tagline":"Microbial crocetin: a controllable cell-factory supplement to saffron farming, taken all the way to B2B ingredient specs","summary":"NFLSHS-China built a fully heterologous crocetin pathway in E. coli (idi, CrtE, CrtB, CrtI, CrtY, CrtZ upstream to zeaxanthin; CsCCD2 plus Neurospora crassa NcALD downstream to free crocetin), then re-engineered it after wet-lab failure from a two-plasmid system into a single-plasmid, two-T7-cassette construct (pET-Ds-Cro) for strain stability under scale-up. They used AlphaFold3, PrankWeb and CB-Dock2 to guide semi-rational mutagenesis of the rate-limiting CsCCD2 enzyme, with the N366G mutant giving a 1.13-fold yield increase confirmed by HPLC. The commercial side is unusually disciplined for a high-school team: a named B2B beachhead, a filed and accepted patent application on the CsCCD2 mutant, two physical MVPs with full formula tables, and an explicit separation of what is designed from what is still unvalidated. They won Best Entrepreneurship and Best Presentation in the High School section.","awards":["Best Presentation — Winner (High School)","Best Entrepreneurship — Winner (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Lean Canvas (problem, solution, UVP, key metrics, customer segments, channels, unfair advantage, cost structure, revenue streams), TAM-SAM-SOM with an explicit refusal to take a percentage of the macro market, SWOT, a risk-assessment matrix, a stage-gated Gantt chart from 2026 to 2032, three named exit options, and Techno-Economic Analysis plus Life Cycle Assessment named as future obligations.","dataviz":"Nineteen numbered entrepreneurship figures: pathway maps for the two-plasmid and single-plasmid constructs, the biomanufacturing-to-application three-stage chain, a capability-gap table, a partnership-network map with a disclaimer about which relationships are confirmed, a risk matrix, a SWOT quadrant and an SDG panel. HPLC chromatograms carry the quantitative claims.","datascience":"HPLC quantification of crocetin as the core analytical readout; mutant screening comparing predicted variants against measured titre (N366G at 1.13x). No fermentation kinetics model or cost model with numbers.","software":"","stack":["AlphaFold3","PrankWeb","CB-Dock2","HPLC","pET-Duet-1 backbone","E. coli BL21(DE3)","E. coli DH5-alpha"],"techniques":["heterologous carotenoid pathway assembly","single-plasmid multi-cassette expression with RBS re-tuning","semi-rational enzyme engineering from binding-pocket prediction","site-directed mutagenesis","vacuum freeze-drying and bead-beating extraction","soft-capsule and essence-oil formulation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"An online survey plus street interviews run 12-17 March 2026 across mixed demographics found over 60 percent of respondents had never heard of crocetin and routinely confused it with crocin, and that over 40 percent would pay only 5-10 RMB per day for a mood-soothing product. That cognitive gap triggered a dedicated education page and a Xiaohongshu (Rednote) account translating biosynthesis into public infographics. They also presented at the 10th iSCRM and the 1st Grand APiC conference, and built peer resources after hearing that team members elsewhere felt unsure their assigned role mattered.","ihp":"Five explicitly staged iHP phases, each written as motivation, action and integration, with dates and named interlocutors. Phase I traced the supply chain to source: an online interview with Iranian saffron grower Ali Norouzi (17 March) produced concrete constraints (15-20 day harvest window, 1-2 g dried saffron per person per day, water shortage, currency volatility), and visits to Daofengtang Rende TCM Clinic and chain pharmacies produced a real price point of 60-90 RMB per gram and the observation that pharmacies barely stock it. Phase II is the strongest: after near-zero crocetin yield on 12 May, Dr Huang Danqiong diagnosed metabolic burden and plasmid instability and told them to collapse to a single plasmid, which they did; Prof Huang Guanqin then reviewed the single-plasmid circuit against scale-up cost on 20 June; Fapon's technical director on 22 July pushed them off blind screening onto AI-guided rational design. Phase III used Associate Professor Li Chenyang twice to kill an unstable aqueous formulation on physicochemical grounds and pick the oil and capsule routes, and a perfumer, Ms Weng, taught the actual blending process. Phase IV took the business plan to Attorney Zhang on 17 September for NMPA/FDA classification, licensing and labelling risk, which changed their claims language across the wiki and social media. This is iHP that visibly rewrote both the construct and the marketing copy.","realWorld":"This is a real venture attempt, not a canvas exercise. The business model is specifically B2B-first: the beachhead is small-to-medium functional skincare brands and boutique cosmetic R&D labs, chosen because they have shorter decision cycles than multinationals, and the first deliverable is explicitly not a consumer product but standardised samples plus HPLC chromatograms to support a customer's own formulation prototyping. Market evidence is real but qualitative: interviews across the full chain (Iranian grower, TCM clinic and pharmacies, boutique formulators, Fapon, an iGEM-mentor founder, a lawyer), a public survey with reported percentages and a willingness-to-pay band, and a visit to Shenzhen University's fermentation facility. They refuse to multiply the roughly USD 2.0 billion TAM by a made-up share and instead say SOM must be computed from reachable R&D nodes times sample volume per test, which is the correct instinct even though they then do not finish the arithmetic. Hard assets exist: a patent application, Carotenoid Cleavage Dioxygenase 2 Mutant, Preparation Method and Application, filed and accepted, and two physical MVPs with published formula tables down to percentages (essence oil at 0.03 percent crocetin, 0.5 percent vitamin E in cold-pressed olive oil; capsule at 70 percent crocetin-olive oil, 15 percent L-theanine, 13 percent soy lecithin, 2 percent vitamin E). The regulatory path is credible and specific, naming NMPA and FDA classification, production and distribution licensing, and the prohibition on medical claims, with a consequence they actually implemented, standardising functional claims site-wide. The continuation plan is a stage-gated Gantt from 2026 lab validation through 2027 customer testing, 2028 CDMO pilot and 2029 B2B commercialisation to 2030-2032 international registration, where each gate has named prerequisite resources and a numeric-in-principle pass condition, plus three exit routes (trade sale to BASF, Givaudan or DSM-Firmenich; technology licensing with royalties; VC/PE buyout). What is genuinely missing is the money: there are no unit economics, no cost of goods, no price per gram, no funding raised, no registered entity and no incubator place, and the partnership map carries its own honest disclaimer that everything except university support and paid service relationships is a prospective contact rather than a formal agreement. They also list, in their own words, the data still absent before industrialisation, including yield, purity, recovery, batch consistency and pilot-scale mutant validation. Even the downside is faced squarely, including the possibility that fermentation's carbon footprint exceeds agriculture's and that cheap biomanufacturing could destroy saffron growers' livelihoods, for which they propose market segmentation that leaves the high-end plant premium alone.","partners":["Fapon (biotech company; technical director and business development; AI platform exposure and commercialisation advice)","Shenzhen University (Prof. Wang Chaogang, Prof. Huang Guanqin, Prof. Chen Weizhao; fermentation facility access)","Dr. Huang Danqiong (fermentation expert; diagnosed the two-plasmid failure)","Associate Professor Li Chenyang (formulation and safety screening)","Ali Norouzi (Iranian saffron grower and supplier)","Daofengtang Rende TCM Clinic and local chain pharmacies","Li Shiyuan (founder, Shanghai Transformant Information Technology Co.; iGEM mentor; technical evaluation)","Attorney Zhang (regulatory and IP legal review)","Ms. Weng (perfumer; essential-oil production process)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship8.avif","caption":"Patent application for the CsCCD2 mutant, filed and accepted, shown on the entrepreneurship page as the project's first hard IP asset","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship10.avif","caption":"Stage-gated development plan from 2026 laboratory validation through 2029 B2B commercialisation to 2030-2032 international expansion","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship9.avif","caption":"Business exit strategy: trade sale to an ingredient major, technology licensing with royalties, or VC/PE buyout","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship17v2.avif","caption":"SWOT analysis supporting the B2B-first ingredient strategy","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy their honesty architecture: a 'Current Validation Status' block that splits completed designs from data still required, and a partnership map annotated to say which relationships are confirmed and which are merely prospective contacts. Pair it with their stage-gate habit, where each phase of the roadmap names its prerequisite resources and the single pass condition that unlocks the next phase, so a project cannot drift forward on enthusiasm.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/product/"],"rank":52}];
